00:00:07.650 --> 00:00:10.720
This week at the round
table of dim lighting,

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:13.237
you'll have to be listening
to me speak like this

00:00:13.237 --> 00:00:15.087
and you know what, did
I bring my tissues in,

00:00:15.087 --> 00:00:16.030
I didn't even bring my frickin' tissues.

00:00:16.030 --> 00:00:16.863
- Oh gosh.

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:20.607
Can you, can somebody help
please get the tissues?

00:00:20.607 --> 00:00:24.070
- There's a tissue box in our office

00:00:24.070 --> 00:00:26.510
which I've been carrying
around with myself all day.

00:00:26.510 --> 00:00:28.087
- Be careful with the dogs,
you'll let the dogs out.

00:00:28.087 --> 00:00:29.950
- Don't let the dogs out.

00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:31.790
- This week at the round
table of dim lighting,

00:00:31.790 --> 00:00:34.470
we are going to get Rhett's tissues

00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:37.820
and hopefully minimize him
talking because the more--

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:39.210
- Minimize me talking?
- Yeah 'cause the more

00:00:39.210 --> 00:00:40.840
you talk, the more I can tell you're sick,

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:44.120
and the more you're spewing
stuff into my radius.

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:46.920
- No no I have been purposely directing it

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:47.960
away from you all day.

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:49.680
I'm currently speaking
out of the right side

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:51.187
of my mouth so it all goes
to one side of the room.

00:00:51.187 --> 00:00:53.010
- Can you face that way?

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:54.020
Are you cool with that?

00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:56.410
- Yeah I'm projecting
everything to this side.

00:00:56.410 --> 00:00:58.370
- But also face that way.

00:00:58.370 --> 00:00:59.300
- Thank you, Kiko.

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:00.850
Look at that, got my tissue box.

00:01:00.850 --> 00:01:02.480
I'll sit it up there.

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:05.760
- Today we're gonna
update you and each other

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:07.960
on the spring break adventures we had.

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:09.516
We're two spring breakers.

00:01:09.516 --> 00:01:10.349
- Yeah.

00:01:10.349 --> 00:01:12.100
- We're not over 40.

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:15.130
We still go on spring
break with our children.

00:01:15.130 --> 00:01:16.400
- Right.

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:18.130
- So yeah we had a full week off

00:01:18.130 --> 00:01:21.540
that we have not fully
downloaded to each other.

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:24.760
I mean it's, from calendar
time it's the distant past,

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:27.590
but in relational time, it's yesterday.

00:01:27.590 --> 00:01:29.370
- Yeah it's been--
- It's yesterday, brother.

00:01:29.370 --> 00:01:30.970
- A week and a half or so.

00:01:30.970 --> 00:01:34.144
We do wanna let you know
that your boys have been

00:01:34.144 --> 00:01:37.660
nominated for a couple of Webbys.

00:01:37.660 --> 00:01:40.880
For your favorite or at least
the podcast you're listening

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:42.340
to right now, Ear Biscuits.

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:45.580
Yes, we are a Webby nominee in podcasts.

00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:48.650
Best host which I think is me but...

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:53.330
- I think that's me.
- Oh really?

00:01:53.330 --> 00:01:55.783
- Yeah, it actually said in parentheses,

00:01:57.195 --> 00:01:59.342
the shorter one.
- So you can go to--

00:01:59.342 --> 00:02:01.380
- The funnier one.
- If you wanna vote for me

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:02.974
or Link as best host--

00:02:02.974 --> 00:02:04.395
- No.

00:02:04.395 --> 00:02:06.012
So we're--
- They don't have a

00:02:06.012 --> 00:02:08.450
best host category.

00:02:08.450 --> 00:02:10.440
- You shouldn't make us
compete against each other.

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:11.810
We're competing against other people.

00:02:11.810 --> 00:02:15.060
- No, the whole idea is
that people compete against,

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:19.020
we compete against each
other to generate more votes

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:21.310
for us collectively 'cause what
we're not telling everybody

00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:22.600
is that a vote for me is a vote for us

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:24.634
and a vote for you is a vote for us.

00:02:24.634 --> 00:02:25.880
So vote twice.

00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:28.600
- You can vote because
there's an audience choice

00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:32.970
and we want you to choose
us, vote.webbyawards.com

00:02:32.970 --> 00:02:35.330
through April 18th, 2019.

00:02:35.330 --> 00:02:37.260
- But that was just one
of the nominations, Link.

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:39.720
The other nomination was just best podcast

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:41.150
in the comedy category.

00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:44.313
- Yeah very honored, thank
you Webbys for the nomination.

00:02:45.580 --> 00:02:48.065
Listen, we don't have much
control over the judge's choices

00:02:48.065 --> 00:02:51.320
but we can encourage you to vote.

00:02:51.320 --> 00:02:52.580
- We are part of the academy.

00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:54.642
- Vote.webbyawards.com.

00:02:54.642 --> 00:02:56.440
- And we will be voting for ourselves.

00:02:56.440 --> 00:02:59.649
- Look only for Ear Biscuits,
and GMM is also nominated.

00:02:59.649 --> 00:03:00.790
- Can you do that, can
you vote for yourself?

00:03:00.790 --> 00:03:04.650
- GMM was nominated for
variety series, comedy, and...

00:03:07.025 --> 00:03:08.760
Is that all we got?

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:10.970
GMM hosts, we got a honorable mention.

00:03:10.970 --> 00:03:12.190
- Yeah yeah we're not good enough--

00:03:12.190 --> 00:03:14.460
- We didn't quite make it.
- Hosts of GMM to be a nominee

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:16.320
but we're good enough to be an honoree.

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:19.280
- And all we do is go on vacations

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:20.469
and come back and talk about it.

00:03:20.469 --> 00:03:21.750
(Rhett laughs)

00:03:21.750 --> 00:03:23.500
I mean and then you call it comedy.

00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:25.350
- That's right.
- It's the new comedy.

00:03:25.350 --> 00:03:26.890
- Hold on, you know what, that's a great--

00:03:26.890 --> 00:03:27.860
- You think it's better than,

00:03:27.860 --> 00:03:29.360
that's not what you call it, Feldman?

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:32.300
- Sorry to touch your sleeve.
- Don't touch my sleeve.

00:03:32.300 --> 00:03:33.507
We're gonna have to desanitize it.

00:03:33.507 --> 00:03:35.840
- But you just actually--
- Well sanitize it.

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:38.870
- You gave me and us--
- A quarter?

00:03:38.870 --> 00:03:42.010
- An idea for what the future
of this podcast could be.

00:03:42.010 --> 00:03:43.766
Let's just be travel buddies

00:03:43.766 --> 00:03:46.261
and just talk about our travels.

00:03:46.261 --> 00:03:48.313
- Yeah, okay.

00:03:49.370 --> 00:03:51.600
Can we go separate?
(Rhett laughs)

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:53.720
Because if we go together
there's nothing to talk about.

00:03:53.720 --> 00:03:54.680
- You're right, yeah,

00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:55.757
yeah I see where you're going with that.

00:03:55.757 --> 00:03:58.010
- Can we be estranged travel buddies?

00:03:58.010 --> 00:03:59.750
- We go, we're not travel buddies,

00:03:59.750 --> 00:04:01.360
we're travel correspondents.

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:02.880
- You need to raise your mic a little bit.

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:05.580
I think Smosh has been
using your mic stand

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:06.980
and you're hunching.

00:04:06.980 --> 00:04:09.500
- You're not supposed to
tell people that Smosh

00:04:09.500 --> 00:04:10.762
records their podcast in here.

00:04:10.762 --> 00:04:12.582
- And you're gonna make 'em sick.

00:04:12.582 --> 00:04:15.400
- Yeah, that's my goal, make Smosh sick.

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:17.320
- That's not strategic for us.

00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:18.730
I'm just gonna be honest.

00:04:18.730 --> 00:04:23.440
So I went back to London for spring break

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:27.570
and we took, I had this idea in January,

00:04:27.570 --> 00:04:31.660
maybe it was the end of last year.

00:04:31.660 --> 00:04:36.660
I had this idea because
Lily, our oldest daughter,

00:04:36.991 --> 00:04:39.810
our oldest child, is turning 16.

00:04:39.810 --> 00:04:42.693
Well she just did, March
31st, she turned 16.

00:04:43.639 --> 00:04:45.940
And I was thinking, wouldn't it be cool

00:04:45.940 --> 00:04:49.154
to just take her on a vacation just us,

00:04:49.154 --> 00:04:52.610
me, Christy and her, so
it's not a family vacation.

00:04:52.610 --> 00:04:56.420
This is like a dedicated time to just

00:04:56.420 --> 00:05:00.180
make it special for her at such
a special point in her life.

00:05:00.180 --> 00:05:02.870
I mean they call it sweet 16.
- For a reason.

00:05:02.870 --> 00:05:04.970
- Well question mark.

00:05:04.970 --> 00:05:09.970
So I would like to explore at
some point how sweet it was.

00:05:10.410 --> 00:05:13.750
There were a couple of acute sour points

00:05:13.750 --> 00:05:15.984
which I will get into.
- Oh, exciting.

00:05:15.984 --> 00:05:20.290
- And it's, I don't know,
I'm still processing.

00:05:20.290 --> 00:05:23.210
Allow me to process with
you so I will give some

00:05:24.260 --> 00:05:26.770
London recommendations
on my second trip there

00:05:26.770 --> 00:05:29.610
in just a number of weeks.

00:05:29.610 --> 00:05:31.240
- And I will use those.

00:05:31.240 --> 00:05:33.700
And I of course went to a different part

00:05:33.700 --> 00:05:36.130
of the world, closer, I
went to Colorado and skied

00:05:36.130 --> 00:05:39.040
and had some harrowing experiences there.

00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:40.507
Wouldn't say I almost
died, at least that I--

00:05:40.507 --> 00:05:43.385
- You had heroin in Vail.

00:05:43.385 --> 00:05:47.915
- At least not as, I didn't
come as close to death,

00:05:47.915 --> 00:05:49.960
you know, sometimes you
never know how close

00:05:49.960 --> 00:05:53.340
you got to death, I mean
maybe I just missed death,

00:05:53.340 --> 00:05:57.560
but I embarrassed myself in
a big way a couple of times.

00:05:57.560 --> 00:05:59.070
- Again, we will know when we die

00:05:59.070 --> 00:06:01.010
and watch the footage back--
- Right.

00:06:01.010 --> 00:06:03.000
- How close you came to death.

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:04.670
I don't know if I'll be there though.

00:06:04.670 --> 00:06:06.650
Travel, estranged travel buddies,

00:06:06.650 --> 00:06:07.650
we're not gonna die at the same time.

00:06:07.650 --> 00:06:09.970
- That's the name of it,
Estranged Travel Buddies

00:06:09.970 --> 00:06:11.550
is the name of the podcast.

00:06:11.550 --> 00:06:13.550
I do wanna tell you about
another travel thing

00:06:13.550 --> 00:06:14.500
that happened to me

00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:18.360
just two days ago.

00:06:18.360 --> 00:06:19.890
So--
- Do it.

00:06:19.890 --> 00:06:23.948
- For those of you who read
the Book of Mythicality,

00:06:23.948 --> 00:06:27.776
or came to the Tour of
Mythicality, you know the story

00:06:27.776 --> 00:06:31.350
that I tell about my
wife's grandmother, Gaga,

00:06:31.350 --> 00:06:36.350
and her integral sort of very funny role

00:06:36.840 --> 00:06:39.030
in our relationship of
emailing back and forth.

00:06:39.030 --> 00:06:41.250
- You should tell a truncated version.

00:06:41.250 --> 00:06:44.460
- Basically she had an email
address back in the 90s

00:06:44.460 --> 00:06:47.770
when I was getting to know
Jessie and Jessie did not

00:06:47.770 --> 00:06:50.280
so I literally emailed her
grandmother who would then

00:06:50.280 --> 00:06:52.720
print out the emails and
then send them to Jessie.

00:06:52.720 --> 00:06:54.240
- Because you were in Slovakia.

00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:55.540
- 'Cause I was in a different place.

00:06:55.540 --> 00:06:58.490
And I tell that story in
the Book of Mythicality

00:06:58.490 --> 00:07:00.960
and on the tour but anyway,

00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:05.114
Gaga at the age of 94
passed away last week.

00:07:05.114 --> 00:07:07.550
Well a little more than a week ago.

00:07:07.550 --> 00:07:08.730
- I was sad to hear that.

00:07:08.730 --> 00:07:12.250
- And we went home for--
- Great woman.

00:07:12.250 --> 00:07:13.890
- A memorial service.

00:07:13.890 --> 00:07:16.100
And I already knew that she
was an incredible woman.

00:07:16.100 --> 00:07:18.200
I knew that she was, like I told you,

00:07:18.200 --> 00:07:23.200
that she was like strangely
great, like unusually unusual.

00:07:25.777 --> 00:07:30.777
She kinda fit the typical
older southern lady

00:07:31.430 --> 00:07:34.720
in a certain number of
ways but she also was just,

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:35.553
the things that you--

00:07:35.553 --> 00:07:37.030
- She would say like southern--

00:07:37.030 --> 00:07:38.360
- She dropped her Rs.
- She talked like--

00:07:38.360 --> 00:07:41.170
- Yeah it was like that old
southern belle kinda thing.

00:07:41.170 --> 00:07:44.120
But like something is up in the ay-ah.

00:07:44.120 --> 00:07:45.320
You know, she literally talked like that

00:07:45.320 --> 00:07:47.490
and she was not joking.
- Yeah.

00:07:47.490 --> 00:07:49.010
- This old Raleigh accent.

00:07:49.010 --> 00:07:51.790
But as we told stories about her, we just,

00:07:51.790 --> 00:07:54.136
all these things came back--

00:07:54.136 --> 00:07:56.900
- Was that the ceremony,
like a storytelling--

00:07:56.900 --> 00:07:59.151
- Yeah it was literally, we just--

00:07:59.151 --> 00:08:00.180
- That's cool.
- All got together

00:08:00.180 --> 00:08:02.840
at her daughter's house, my mother-in-law,

00:08:02.840 --> 00:08:06.610
and we just told stories about
her and we all wore white.

00:08:06.610 --> 00:08:08.240
She was like I don't
want y'all to wear black.

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:11.600
I want you to wear white
'cause it's a celebration.

00:08:11.600 --> 00:08:14.830
I lived 94 years, and so anyway, it was,

00:08:14.830 --> 00:08:17.610
there was lots of laughs, lots
of funny stories about Gaga,

00:08:17.610 --> 00:08:18.810
lots of tears at the same time.

00:08:18.810 --> 00:08:22.041
But anyway I had to make a very quick trip

00:08:22.041 --> 00:08:24.053
to North Carolina for the weekend.

00:08:25.120 --> 00:08:26.923
Took the red eye on Friday night.

00:08:28.210 --> 00:08:31.300
There's actually an airport in New Bern.

00:08:31.300 --> 00:08:32.580
- Yeah.
- Which is not too far

00:08:32.580 --> 00:08:34.140
from where your wife grew up.

00:08:34.140 --> 00:08:34.980
- Down east.

00:08:34.980 --> 00:08:37.780
- And that's a lot closer, 'cause we,

00:08:37.780 --> 00:08:40.000
it was actually at my in-law's beach house

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:42.090
is where we had the
ceremony so I was like well,

00:08:42.090 --> 00:08:46.340
this is a quick trip, I'm
gonna fly directly to New Bern

00:08:46.340 --> 00:08:49.010
and then rent a car and
go down to the beach house

00:08:49.010 --> 00:08:51.660
and I was gonna fly, flew on Friday night.

00:08:51.660 --> 00:08:52.950
- We filmed all day.

00:08:52.950 --> 00:08:56.280
- Yeah yeah yeah, so
yeah, we were shooting GMM

00:08:56.280 --> 00:09:00.580
and some other things on Friday
and so we went pretty late

00:09:00.580 --> 00:09:03.230
so I took that red eye
which incidentally was one

00:09:03.230 --> 00:09:05.927
of the red eyes where I was
hoping that I was gonna have

00:09:05.927 --> 00:09:08.560
a little bit more room
but I basically just sat

00:09:08.560 --> 00:09:10.950
in an upright position and tried to sleep

00:09:10.950 --> 00:09:12.500
which was not fun, it's not fun

00:09:12.500 --> 00:09:14.200
for a big man to have to do that.

00:09:14.200 --> 00:09:15.360
- Tell that to a horse.

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:18.240
- Believe it's one of the
reasons that I got sick

00:09:18.240 --> 00:09:19.360
'cause I didn't get a lot of sleep

00:09:19.360 --> 00:09:21.753
and I was already getting sick, anyway,

00:09:23.513 --> 00:09:27.040
beautiful ceremony on
Saturday and then Sunday

00:09:27.040 --> 00:09:28.830
we're gonna go back and of course we have

00:09:28.830 --> 00:09:30.490
to go to the New Bern airport.

00:09:30.490 --> 00:09:31.960
Then you take a flight from New Bern

00:09:31.960 --> 00:09:33.360
to Charlotte, North Carolina--

00:09:33.360 --> 00:09:34.910
- Like a puddle jumper situation?

00:09:34.910 --> 00:09:38.010
- I mean it wasn't, yeah,
it was a small, small plane.

00:09:38.010 --> 00:09:40.210
Bigger than I anticipated
but still pretty small.

00:09:40.210 --> 00:09:45.210
One row of seats and then
two rows, one column of seats

00:09:45.370 --> 00:09:48.713
and then two columns of seats
on each side of the plane.

00:09:49.580 --> 00:09:52.230
And so we get to the New Bern airport

00:09:52.230 --> 00:09:56.370
which incidentally is like two gates

00:09:56.370 --> 00:09:59.580
and there are--
- Like a strip mall

00:09:59.580 --> 00:10:00.930
type situation?
- It looks like you're pulling

00:10:00.930 --> 00:10:03.310
into a retirement home.
- Oh.

00:10:03.310 --> 00:10:04.143
Even better.

00:10:04.143 --> 00:10:05.320
- That's what the airport is like.

00:10:05.320 --> 00:10:08.930
Like literally the rental car
drop off is just a parking lot

00:10:08.930 --> 00:10:10.750
and it's for all the companies.

00:10:10.750 --> 00:10:14.810
And then when you go
inside, no matter what

00:10:14.810 --> 00:10:18.630
the rental car company is, you
deal with the Alamo person.

00:10:18.630 --> 00:10:21.950
So there's a sign on the
Enterprise thing that says,

00:10:21.950 --> 00:10:24.820
we're out, please talk
to Alamo. (chuckles)

00:10:24.820 --> 00:10:26.370
- Oh wow.
- So like,

00:10:26.370 --> 00:10:29.240
they got one employee
handling all the rental cars.

00:10:29.240 --> 00:10:31.510
There's rocking chairs in the
airport which incidentally

00:10:31.510 --> 00:10:33.560
there's rocking chairs
in the Charlotte airport.

00:10:33.560 --> 00:10:35.950
But it's much more quaint
than the New Bern airport

00:10:35.950 --> 00:10:38.610
which I think is called Coastal
Carolina Regional Airport.

00:10:38.610 --> 00:10:40.921
- Are you sure you weren't
flying out of a Cracker Barrel?

00:10:40.921 --> 00:10:42.908
- (chuckles) It was as
much like a Cracker Barrel

00:10:42.908 --> 00:10:44.710
as an airport can be.

00:10:44.710 --> 00:10:46.320
- Okay.

00:10:46.320 --> 00:10:49.250
I hope to be successful enough
to park my private plane

00:10:49.250 --> 00:10:51.810
at Cracker Barrels.
- It's so small

00:10:51.810 --> 00:10:54.820
that you don't just get there
and then go through security,

00:10:54.820 --> 00:10:56.960
you get there and you sit outside

00:10:56.960 --> 00:10:59.150
and then right before
your plane takes off,

00:10:59.150 --> 00:11:01.300
they're like okay, everybody
go through security.

00:11:01.300 --> 00:11:02.600
Then you go through security
and just basically get

00:11:02.600 --> 00:11:04.573
on the plane, that's how small it is.

00:11:06.270 --> 00:11:08.760
So Locke and I get there,

00:11:08.760 --> 00:11:11.420
I got there like an hour before the flight

00:11:11.420 --> 00:11:13.660
which is very late for me typically,

00:11:13.660 --> 00:11:14.493
you know what I'm saying,

00:11:14.493 --> 00:11:17.360
I'm at 1.5 to two hours ahead of time

00:11:17.360 --> 00:11:19.014
but I knew that we didn't
have a lot to deal with.

00:11:19.014 --> 00:11:20.890
- And you're still an anxious wreck.

00:11:20.890 --> 00:11:24.900
- And I get there an hour
early and then I realize

00:11:24.900 --> 00:11:28.330
that the flight has been
delayed by 30 minutes

00:11:28.330 --> 00:11:30.190
which is gonna give us about 30 minutes

00:11:30.190 --> 00:11:31.570
to make our connection.

00:11:31.570 --> 00:11:34.870
From the time the doors,
we touch down in Charlotte.

00:11:34.870 --> 00:11:37.770
We got about 30 minutes
before they shut the door,

00:11:37.770 --> 00:11:39.130
so I'm a little anxious.

00:11:39.130 --> 00:11:42.300
Anything could go wrong
here, sitting there waiting,

00:11:42.300 --> 00:11:43.720
and then the flight gets delayed.

00:11:43.720 --> 00:11:46.278
Now we get onto the runway

00:11:46.278 --> 00:11:49.057
and the pilot says, "I, unfortunate news,

00:11:49.057 --> 00:11:51.107
"we've been given a weather delay.

00:11:51.107 --> 00:11:53.860
"We're gonna take off in
approximately 30 minutes."

00:11:53.860 --> 00:11:54.693
- That's not gonna work.

00:11:54.693 --> 00:11:56.089
- So basically my entire buffer--

00:11:56.089 --> 00:11:57.553
- Yeah.
- Is gone.

00:11:59.000 --> 00:12:00.400
So first thing I do--
- Could have stayed

00:12:00.400 --> 00:12:02.020
in that rocking chair.
- First thing I do

00:12:02.020 --> 00:12:06.300
because I have become
basically totally dependent

00:12:06.300 --> 00:12:08.355
on people helping me do things
at this point in my life--

00:12:08.355 --> 00:12:09.460
(Link chuckles)

00:12:09.460 --> 00:12:13.640
Is I text Jenna, my
assistant, our assistant.

00:12:13.640 --> 00:12:15.473
- Oh gosh.
- And I'm like, Jenna.

00:12:16.568 --> 00:12:17.920
(sighs)

00:12:17.920 --> 00:12:19.430
Here's the situation,
I actually called her.

00:12:19.430 --> 00:12:21.970
I called her, I was like,
okay, we were supposed

00:12:21.970 --> 00:12:24.733
to have a buffer, we're not
gonna have a buffer and--

00:12:24.733 --> 00:12:26.840
- I just need a shoulder
to cry on right now.

00:12:26.840 --> 00:12:27.990
- I need you to--
- Through the phone.

00:12:27.990 --> 00:12:30.460
- I need you to get me on the next flight.

00:12:30.460 --> 00:12:33.760
I talked to the woman at the
gate in New Bern preemptively

00:12:33.760 --> 00:12:35.630
and she was like well the
next flight's completely full.

00:12:35.630 --> 00:12:37.517
You could do standby or you
could fly out in the morning

00:12:37.517 --> 00:12:39.140
and I'm just thinking about the fact

00:12:39.140 --> 00:12:41.920
that I don't wanna be there,
I'm with Locke by the way

00:12:41.920 --> 00:12:43.700
so Locke and I flew together 'cause Jessie

00:12:43.700 --> 00:12:46.530
and Shepherd were staying
a couple days extra.

00:12:46.530 --> 00:12:47.780
Locke's gotta get back to school,

00:12:47.780 --> 00:12:50.010
I gotta get back to work,
we got things happenin',

00:12:50.010 --> 00:12:52.318
we got things scheduled,
we got meetings, et cetera.

00:12:52.318 --> 00:12:54.060
- You're important enough
to have an assistant.

00:12:54.060 --> 00:12:56.323
- And so,

00:12:57.160 --> 00:13:00.160
she was like okay I'll
get on the phone with the,

00:13:00.160 --> 00:13:01.940
actually the representative
is supposed to call me back

00:13:01.940 --> 00:13:04.120
in 50 minutes because that's what,

00:13:04.120 --> 00:13:05.560
when she called the
number, they were like,

00:13:05.560 --> 00:13:09.040
to get a call back 50 minutes
from now, so she did that.

00:13:09.040 --> 00:13:11.170
And that's about how long the flight takes

00:13:11.170 --> 00:13:14.270
so we touch down, mathematically speaking,

00:13:14.270 --> 00:13:16.050
and I had talked to the pilot.

00:13:16.050 --> 00:13:17.670
I'd gone up to the two pilots

00:13:17.670 --> 00:13:19.560
'cause everybody's sitting with each other

00:13:19.560 --> 00:13:20.980
and I start talking with them with like--

00:13:20.980 --> 00:13:23.210
- Hold on, you were
sittin' with the pilots.

00:13:23.210 --> 00:13:25.240
- The pilots were sitting
waiting to get on the plane.

00:13:25.240 --> 00:13:26.073
- Oh.
- Waiting to get on the plane

00:13:26.073 --> 00:13:28.660
and I was like hey guys,
how confident are you

00:13:28.660 --> 00:13:31.780
in this getting to Charlotte?

00:13:31.780 --> 00:13:34.110
And they were like younger than me and so,

00:13:34.110 --> 00:13:37.560
and they were like, well, you know,

00:13:37.560 --> 00:13:39.230
it'll probably be one hour total

00:13:39.230 --> 00:13:43.020
from closing the doors
to opening the doors.

00:13:43.020 --> 00:13:44.810
Getting off the next
plane and they were like,

00:13:44.810 --> 00:13:45.715
where you gotta get?

00:13:45.715 --> 00:13:47.420
I was like I got a really
tight connection to LAX

00:13:47.420 --> 00:13:48.800
and they're like okay
we'll do whatever we can

00:13:48.800 --> 00:13:50.162
to get there as fast as we, like,

00:13:50.162 --> 00:13:51.590
I don't know what kind
of control they have.

00:13:51.590 --> 00:13:53.267
I thought it was all autopilot anyway.

00:13:53.267 --> 00:13:54.523
- What'd you think, they
might say, you know what,

00:13:54.523 --> 00:13:56.750
we'll just take you to Los Angeles.

00:13:56.750 --> 00:13:57.880
- Yeah I was hoping for that.

00:13:57.880 --> 00:13:59.060
- I've seen you on the internet.

00:13:59.060 --> 00:13:59.893
- We'll reroute the plane.

00:13:59.893 --> 00:14:01.708
- I think you're nominated
for a Webby, hey,

00:14:01.708 --> 00:14:03.160
do you have an assistant?

00:14:03.160 --> 00:14:03.993
- That was what I was hoping for secretly.

00:14:03.993 --> 00:14:05.810
- All right we're going
straight to Los Angeles.

00:14:05.810 --> 00:14:07.480
- I was hoping they would
secretly recognize me

00:14:07.480 --> 00:14:09.340
and make accommodations
but that didn't happen.

00:14:09.340 --> 00:14:12.774
So anyway, I was freaking
out as you might imagine

00:14:12.774 --> 00:14:17.510
and then we, our flight out
of Charlotte was at 5:45.

00:14:17.510 --> 00:14:21.560
The plane lands, now we've
been told they close the doors

00:14:21.560 --> 00:14:24.970
10 minutes before departure,
5:35, the doors will close.

00:14:24.970 --> 00:14:28.760
We landed, we touched down
on the runway at 5:25.

00:14:28.760 --> 00:14:31.350
It took seven minutes for them to--

00:14:31.350 --> 00:14:33.030
- You got three minutes to get there.

00:14:33.030 --> 00:14:35.330
- But not only that, because
the plane is so small,

00:14:35.330 --> 00:14:37.200
we had to valet our bags

00:14:37.200 --> 00:14:39.306
so we couldn't just get off the plane.

00:14:39.306 --> 00:14:41.950
They had to put the bags
with the tags underneath,

00:14:41.950 --> 00:14:43.400
gate check the bags.
- Okay.

00:14:44.290 --> 00:14:45.479
- So--
- How much time did you have

00:14:45.479 --> 00:14:47.670
when you got your bags?

00:14:47.670 --> 00:14:49.140
- One minute.

00:14:49.140 --> 00:14:50.650
- Okay.
- We had one minute.

00:14:50.650 --> 00:14:53.240
We got our bags at 5:34.

00:14:53.240 --> 00:14:56.464
I'm texting Jenna, she's
like, the woman says

00:14:56.464 --> 00:14:58.110
that you're not gonna make it.

00:14:58.110 --> 00:15:00.720
She's talked to the gate,
they're not holding.

00:15:00.720 --> 00:15:02.600
They're not holding it for you.

00:15:02.600 --> 00:15:05.100
That's what Jenna's saying
that the American Airlines

00:15:05.100 --> 00:15:06.250
woman is saying to her.

00:15:07.511 --> 00:15:09.007
She's like I've already got
you booked on the next flight.

00:15:09.007 --> 00:15:11.320
- I think they did know who you were.

00:15:11.320 --> 00:15:13.950
- They've already got you
booked on the next flight.

00:15:13.950 --> 00:15:17.468
So first of all, I looked at
a map of the Charlotte airport

00:15:17.468 --> 00:15:20.180
and I was all the way
at the end of terminal D

00:15:20.180 --> 00:15:22.712
or E, I had to go all the way down E

00:15:22.712 --> 00:15:23.545
and then back up D.
- You're trying to

00:15:23.545 --> 00:15:24.440
Jason Bourne this thing.

00:15:24.440 --> 00:15:26.680
- All the way down E and back up D.

00:15:26.680 --> 00:15:28.460
And I'm looking at the
thing, I'm like okay,

00:15:28.460 --> 00:15:30.330
maybe I can make this.

00:15:30.330 --> 00:15:31.940
I was thinking maybe I could
make this in five minutes

00:15:31.940 --> 00:15:34.730
when I had one minute, I was already sick.

00:15:34.730 --> 00:15:38.156
Anyway, Locke and I pick up our carry ons.

00:15:38.156 --> 00:15:40.500
We're literally carrying our carry ons

00:15:40.500 --> 00:15:41.820
and we both have backpacks.

00:15:41.820 --> 00:15:44.260
We start running, Locke's a young man.

00:15:44.260 --> 00:15:46.360
He's 15, he's in shape.

00:15:46.360 --> 00:15:48.563
He kinda gets ahead of me pretty quickly.

00:15:49.770 --> 00:15:52.420
I'm running as fast as I possibly can

00:15:52.420 --> 00:15:54.570
without hurting myself
through the Charlotte airport.

00:15:54.570 --> 00:15:55.857
- Wow.
- Saying, "Excuse me,

00:15:55.857 --> 00:15:58.530
"excuse me, excuse me,
excuse me, excuse me."

00:15:58.530 --> 00:16:00.639
Being as polite as possible,
still a good southern boy.

00:16:00.639 --> 00:16:02.740
- I would have just resorted to a beep.

00:16:02.740 --> 00:16:03.853
Boop, boop, boop!

00:16:04.690 --> 00:16:06.040
- Then they just think you're backing up.

00:16:06.040 --> 00:16:08.350
They think, "Excuse the
cart," when you do that

00:16:08.350 --> 00:16:09.183
and then they'd get out

00:16:09.183 --> 00:16:10.016
of the way slowly.
- They would have

00:16:10.016 --> 00:16:10.849
looked for a cart.

00:16:10.849 --> 00:16:12.100
- Just flying through the airport.

00:16:12.100 --> 00:16:14.030
Eventually I had to
stop and gather myself.

00:16:14.030 --> 00:16:15.680
- Oh.
- I mean it was that crazy.

00:16:15.680 --> 00:16:17.340
- Let's take a breather,
hands on the knees.

00:16:17.340 --> 00:16:20.270
- Multiple, so in the
end I stopped three times

00:16:20.270 --> 00:16:21.947
and we get all the way to the gate

00:16:21.947 --> 00:16:24.290
and I see a bunch of people
lined up and I'm like

00:16:24.290 --> 00:16:27.460
this is potentially really good news?

00:16:27.460 --> 00:16:31.393
And then the guy sees us
run up, this dude is like,

00:16:32.410 --> 00:16:35.417
they changed it to six
o'clock guys, no worries.

00:16:35.417 --> 00:16:36.750
- Shh, really?

00:16:36.750 --> 00:16:39.010
- They moved it back 15 minutes
so they had already boarded

00:16:39.010 --> 00:16:43.560
my group so I'm like sweating profusely.

00:16:43.560 --> 00:16:46.720
I've got a hoodie on that
I've worn and ran in.

00:16:46.720 --> 00:16:47.553
- Oh gosh.

00:16:47.553 --> 00:16:51.330
- And anyway, I think
that sent me even further

00:16:51.330 --> 00:16:55.810
into sickness because I just
completely exerted myself.

00:16:55.810 --> 00:16:58.160
That's the most I've worked
out in probably a decade,

00:16:58.160 --> 00:17:00.131
it was just to run across
the Charlotte airport

00:17:00.131 --> 00:17:02.157
and you know what, I could have strolled

00:17:02.157 --> 00:17:04.560
'cause I had 15 minutes instead of one.

00:17:04.560 --> 00:17:06.850
- I would have said, you know what,

00:17:06.850 --> 00:17:09.640
I'd rather not worry about
this and I wouldn't have done,

00:17:09.640 --> 00:17:12.060
I mean I'm not rubbing it
in I'm just telling you,

00:17:12.060 --> 00:17:13.610
I would have not done anything.

00:17:13.610 --> 00:17:15.769
I would have been like well,

00:17:15.769 --> 00:17:17.680
I'll get there when I get there.

00:17:17.680 --> 00:17:18.987
But ironically I would have gotten there

00:17:18.987 --> 00:17:19.820
the same time as you.
- And nine times

00:17:19.820 --> 00:17:21.090
out of 10, you would
have missed the flight.

00:17:21.090 --> 00:17:22.390
- Oh that's your opinion.

00:17:22.390 --> 00:17:23.530
- And I would have missed the flight.

00:17:23.530 --> 00:17:24.363
If they hadn't moved the flight--

00:17:24.363 --> 00:17:25.376
- If I was there we would--
- I would have missed it.

00:17:25.376 --> 00:17:27.110
- Have gotten in a huge fight.

00:17:27.110 --> 00:17:29.230
I would have just, I
probably wouldn't have ran.

00:17:29.230 --> 00:17:30.430
- I would have gotten on
the plane without you.

00:17:30.430 --> 00:17:32.483
- Yeah I probably wouldn't have ran.

00:17:32.483 --> 00:17:33.536
I probably would have ran

00:17:33.536 --> 00:17:35.530
but I would have been very angry about it

00:17:35.530 --> 00:17:38.860
and then I would have been
proven right when we got there.

00:17:38.860 --> 00:17:41.810
- What did Locke say, was he mad?

00:17:41.810 --> 00:17:44.960
- No, he was excited, it was an adventure.

00:17:44.960 --> 00:17:46.550
- Well he's not 41.

00:17:46.550 --> 00:17:48.475
He didn't almost pull something or die.

00:17:48.475 --> 00:17:50.820
- Kiko, could you grab that
trash can so I don't have

00:17:50.820 --> 00:17:54.770
to throw my snot-laden
tissues onto the floor?

00:17:54.770 --> 00:17:57.170
- And then Jenna did all
this work for nothing.

00:17:57.170 --> 00:17:59.720
Now Kiko's doing this, look at you, man.

00:17:59.720 --> 00:18:00.666
Look at you, man.

00:18:00.666 --> 00:18:01.750
(Rhett sighs)

00:18:01.750 --> 00:18:03.290
- Anyway--
- Can't get your own Kleenex,

00:18:03.290 --> 00:18:04.620
can't get your own trash can.

00:18:04.620 --> 00:18:07.273
- If you need the directions from gate E38

00:18:08.750 --> 00:18:11.900
to gate D10 at Charlotte
just hit me up on Twitter.

00:18:11.900 --> 00:18:13.240
That's @rhettmc.

00:18:13.240 --> 00:18:15.770
- I bet you smelled horrible.

00:18:15.770 --> 00:18:17.400
- No I smelled good.
- You actually didn't.

00:18:17.400 --> 00:18:19.620
- My sweat tends to generate good odors.

00:18:19.620 --> 00:18:21.260
- Oh gosh, listen at you man.

00:18:21.260 --> 00:18:22.980
Listen atcha.
- Listen at you.

00:18:22.980 --> 00:18:26.273
- I'm gonna share my experience
and then hear about yours.

00:18:27.200 --> 00:18:31.573
But first, we gotta promote
some stuff, some merch.

00:18:32.610 --> 00:18:35.510
Mythical.store, it's where you
can get all types of stuff.

00:18:36.750 --> 00:18:37.943
Big and small.

00:18:38.820 --> 00:18:40.380
Here's a small thing.

00:18:40.380 --> 00:18:44.528
A popsocket that says, it's
got the Mythical logo on it.

00:18:44.528 --> 00:18:46.300
- I got a popsocket--
- That's our company.

00:18:46.300 --> 00:18:48.780
- That has the Good
Mythical Morning logo on it.

00:18:48.780 --> 00:18:50.390
- So if you want a little something like

00:18:50.390 --> 00:18:53.550
a mid-year stocking stuffer,

00:18:53.550 --> 00:18:58.135
like get the jump on
stuffing that socking.

00:18:58.135 --> 00:19:00.570
At Mythical.store.

00:19:00.570 --> 00:19:02.680
You'll forget that it was in there.

00:19:02.680 --> 00:19:04.007
You're like wow, I've already, fill it up.

00:19:04.007 --> 00:19:07.090
- Listen to that satisfying
sound that a popsocket makes.

00:19:07.090 --> 00:19:09.760
- Fill it halfway up. (popsocket pops)

00:19:09.760 --> 00:19:14.633
We got stickers, we got all
types of stuff, rep ya boys.

00:19:15.570 --> 00:19:17.160
That's okay, we didn't need that anymore.

00:19:17.160 --> 00:19:17.993
- Sorry.

00:19:19.363 --> 00:19:21.160
- (sighs) Anything else you wanna promote?

00:19:21.160 --> 00:19:23.240
We got a store on Amazon
too, you can check that out.

00:19:23.240 --> 00:19:25.630
It's got different types of t-shirts, psh.

00:19:25.630 --> 00:19:28.483
All types of stuff happenin', yeah.

00:19:29.820 --> 00:19:30.653
Okay.

00:19:33.600 --> 00:19:37.830
I think our spring break
vacations were like polar opposite

00:19:37.830 --> 00:19:41.660
because I did something
that I'm not used to doing

00:19:41.660 --> 00:19:45.361
and then this will play out,
me not being used to this

00:19:45.361 --> 00:19:47.500
over the course of the
specifics of my story

00:19:47.500 --> 00:19:50.700
but we didn't go on
what I call a vacation,

00:19:50.700 --> 00:19:53.130
we went on a sight-seeing trip of London.

00:19:53.130 --> 00:19:56.053
Lily had the choice, she
wanted to go to London.

00:19:56.929 --> 00:20:01.929
I think predominantly because
she's a Harry Potter fan.

00:20:03.588 --> 00:20:06.530
And then, I just think she's a fan

00:20:06.530 --> 00:20:09.800
of like British programming.

00:20:09.800 --> 00:20:12.090
I mean she went through a Doctor Who phase

00:20:12.090 --> 00:20:16.980
and the Sherlock Benedict
Cumberbatch type situation.

00:20:16.980 --> 00:20:20.500
So I can't fault her for liking

00:20:20.500 --> 00:20:25.410
that British comedic sensibility
and storytelling panache.

00:20:27.600 --> 00:20:29.770
So I was like, you
know, I missed the whole

00:20:29.770 --> 00:20:33.530
Harry Potter boat but
maybe there isn't a boat,

00:20:33.530 --> 00:20:34.680
I don't know.
- There's probably a boat

00:20:34.680 --> 00:20:36.705
in one of the stories.
- They don't have any boats

00:20:36.705 --> 00:20:37.773
in Harry Potter I don't think.

00:20:37.773 --> 00:20:39.700
They got broomsticks.
- You gotta have a boat

00:20:39.700 --> 00:20:42.100
for something.
- Just broomsticks.

00:20:42.100 --> 00:20:45.446
And a train, trains and sticks, not boats.

00:20:45.446 --> 00:20:46.590
- Oh.

00:20:46.590 --> 00:20:50.570
- But I'm like, you know
what, this is for you.

00:20:50.570 --> 00:20:53.440
Okay, we knew she was gonna choose London.

00:20:53.440 --> 00:20:58.440
I basically booked the trip,
got the Airbnb in Lisson Grove

00:20:58.970 --> 00:21:01.410
which is an area of London,
it's pretty centrally-located,

00:21:01.410 --> 00:21:02.973
close to the Tube.
- Like Listen Grove?

00:21:02.973 --> 00:21:05.370
- L-I-S-S-O-N.

00:21:05.370 --> 00:21:10.210
And you know so a nice little
Airbnb, a little bed for her

00:21:10.210 --> 00:21:12.300
and then another bedroom
for me and Christy.

00:21:12.300 --> 00:21:14.313
Full kitchen, all this type of vibe.

00:21:14.313 --> 00:21:16.967
So we got our own space.

00:21:16.967 --> 00:21:21.752
But it was much more a
sight-seeing trip than a vacation,

00:21:21.752 --> 00:21:23.690
'cause there's no relaxing.

00:21:23.690 --> 00:21:24.970
It's like what are we gonna do next

00:21:24.970 --> 00:21:26.370
and what are we gonna see next?

00:21:26.370 --> 00:21:31.065
We had booked going to the
Warner Bros. Studio Tour

00:21:31.065 --> 00:21:35.690
where they filmed all
the Harry Potter movies

00:21:35.690 --> 00:21:40.690
and they have all the
actual props and costuming

00:21:42.770 --> 00:21:47.114
and detailed set pieces
and all types of things

00:21:47.114 --> 00:21:49.423
from all of the movies.

00:21:50.480 --> 00:21:52.470
So we booked that ahead of time.

00:21:52.470 --> 00:21:57.470
On another day we also booked
tickets to the stage play

00:21:58.050 --> 00:22:00.943
from the world of Harry
Potter called Cursed Child.

00:22:01.847 --> 00:22:03.791
Which I remember a couple of years back,

00:22:03.791 --> 00:22:06.900
she bought the, we bought her the script.

00:22:06.900 --> 00:22:08.470
I was like okay is this
another Harry Potter book?

00:22:08.470 --> 00:22:09.773
No it's just the script.

00:22:11.690 --> 00:22:14.150
So we got our hands on tickets for that.

00:22:14.150 --> 00:22:16.537
There's a two hour show
and then there's a break,

00:22:16.537 --> 00:22:18.710
I mean there's intermission
in the middle of that

00:22:18.710 --> 00:22:20.820
and then you come back
later and there's two,

00:22:20.820 --> 00:22:24.090
basically two more hours so
it's two different plays.

00:22:24.090 --> 00:22:25.060
- That's a lot of play.

00:22:25.060 --> 00:22:28.340
- But it's one story so
you gotta kinda go all in.

00:22:28.340 --> 00:22:31.450
- It was about like two
hours too much play for me.

00:22:31.450 --> 00:22:33.450
- I was nervous.

00:22:33.450 --> 00:22:35.880
- Maybe 2.5 hours too much.

00:22:35.880 --> 00:22:38.940
- I mean I may come back
to it but I will say that

00:22:39.820 --> 00:22:41.700
having done everything
that I could list out

00:22:41.700 --> 00:22:45.650
that we did, it was a highlight.

00:22:45.650 --> 00:22:47.618
I really enjoyed it, it's
one of my favorite days

00:22:47.618 --> 00:22:49.590
was when we did that.

00:22:49.590 --> 00:22:54.210
We saw the first play, first two hours,

00:22:54.210 --> 00:22:55.700
and then we took a break, walked around

00:22:55.700 --> 00:22:57.840
and we went to Dishoom.

00:22:59.480 --> 00:23:00.923
Our favorite place when we went to London.

00:23:00.923 --> 00:23:03.310
- Very good.
- I went to a different one

00:23:03.310 --> 00:23:05.500
and it was just as amazing.

00:23:05.500 --> 00:23:08.895
I am actually calling it
my favorite restaurant now.

00:23:08.895 --> 00:23:10.460
- You're calling it your
favorite restaurant.

00:23:10.460 --> 00:23:11.330
- Yeah I mean it's--

00:23:11.330 --> 00:23:12.593
- Better than Slug and Lettuce?

00:23:12.593 --> 00:23:14.200
- Yeah.

00:23:14.200 --> 00:23:15.450
And then we went back--
- Whoa.

00:23:15.450 --> 00:23:19.920
- For the other part of it
and it was really well done,

00:23:19.920 --> 00:23:21.283
it wasn't a musical.

00:23:22.870 --> 00:23:26.430
Which, I think side stepped
a bunch of potential

00:23:27.580 --> 00:23:29.455
pot holes so to speak.
- There's that much time,

00:23:29.455 --> 00:23:32.190
oh intermission such that
you can eat a dinner?

00:23:32.190 --> 00:23:33.023
- No.

00:23:33.023 --> 00:23:35.510
There was intermission in the
middle of the two hour play

00:23:35.510 --> 00:23:38.020
and then there were like, you
go out for two and a half,

00:23:38.020 --> 00:23:39.180
three hours, then you come back

00:23:39.180 --> 00:23:41.024
and watch another two hour play.

00:23:41.024 --> 00:23:43.851
- Right so it's a long break.

00:23:43.851 --> 00:23:47.240
- Yeah, the intermission was
just a 15 minute bathroom break

00:23:47.240 --> 00:23:48.860
in the middle of each play.

00:23:48.860 --> 00:23:50.058
- Got it.

00:23:50.058 --> 00:23:51.890
- Two part story.
- Yeah so that,

00:23:51.890 --> 00:23:55.080
I mean, day three was our studio tour.

00:23:55.080 --> 00:23:59.280
Day four was Cursed Child, so day one,

00:23:59.280 --> 00:24:01.180
just to kinda rattle
off the things we did,

00:24:01.180 --> 00:24:05.530
we took the Tube to Westminster
Abbey but it was a Sunday

00:24:05.530 --> 00:24:07.620
so we couldn't get inside,
I didn't realize that

00:24:07.620 --> 00:24:09.490
so I'm like ooh, that's my bad.

00:24:09.490 --> 00:24:11.040
It started hailing.

00:24:11.040 --> 00:24:12.810
It was cold.

00:24:12.810 --> 00:24:16.330
Like literally, it was the
only day that I saw sun

00:24:16.330 --> 00:24:17.957
the whole time I was there.

00:24:17.957 --> 00:24:20.720
I was like that is a
scarce commodity in London.

00:24:20.720 --> 00:24:23.910
The solar thing, solar ball in the sky.

00:24:23.910 --> 00:24:25.060
- Solar ball in the sky.

00:24:25.060 --> 00:24:27.967
- The only day I saw it,
it was also hailing on me.

00:24:27.967 --> 00:24:31.120
So I had to adjust my
plan instead of like going

00:24:31.120 --> 00:24:32.743
to Piccadilly Circus and,

00:24:33.690 --> 00:24:36.300
we missed the changing of
the guard, got hailed on.

00:24:36.300 --> 00:24:40.318
I'm like man, this is not a
good start on a first full day.

00:24:40.318 --> 00:24:42.742
And then I was like it's too cold.

00:24:42.742 --> 00:24:45.350
I was like we gotta go
into the British Museum.

00:24:45.350 --> 00:24:48.560
Saw the Rosetta Stone,
which, that's pretty awesome.

00:24:48.560 --> 00:24:52.090
I didn't realize that
according to what I read there,

00:24:52.090 --> 00:24:55.940
at least, maybe they're
puffing themselves up too much

00:24:55.940 --> 00:24:59.815
but if it wasn't for the Rosetta Stone,

00:24:59.815 --> 00:25:04.815
we would not know how to
translate Egyptian hieroglyphics.

00:25:05.520 --> 00:25:06.353
Period.

00:25:06.353 --> 00:25:08.240
- We also wouldn't have
those great infomercials late

00:25:08.240 --> 00:25:09.250
at night about--

00:25:09.250 --> 00:25:10.090
- Which is why they exist right?

00:25:10.090 --> 00:25:11.307
- The software.

00:25:11.307 --> 00:25:14.026
- There's three different
languages on there

00:25:14.026 --> 00:25:17.090
and then each language says
that hey, this is written

00:25:17.090 --> 00:25:19.490
in two other languages, it
says this is the same thing

00:25:19.490 --> 00:25:20.590
written in three different languages,

00:25:20.590 --> 00:25:22.910
so whichever language
you read, you know that.

00:25:22.910 --> 00:25:27.910
And that's how they
deciphered hieroglyphics.

00:25:29.300 --> 00:25:33.800
I also saw one of the Easter Island dudes.

00:25:33.800 --> 00:25:35.430
They just got sitting in there.

00:25:35.430 --> 00:25:36.860
- The British--
- That was pretty cool.

00:25:36.860 --> 00:25:39.270
- The British have had a
tendency to just go places

00:25:39.270 --> 00:25:41.320
and steal things and then bring
'em back to their museums.

00:25:41.320 --> 00:25:43.388
- There's nothing British
in the British Museum.

00:25:43.388 --> 00:25:44.610
(Rhett laughs)

00:25:44.610 --> 00:25:46.853
I mean there's like
hunks of the Parthenon.

00:25:47.870 --> 00:25:51.610
It was awesome, but yeah you
do get this weird feeling

00:25:51.610 --> 00:25:55.493
and like, I heard people joking about it,

00:25:58.400 --> 00:26:01.400
about how nothing there is
British and I guess I mean,

00:26:01.400 --> 00:26:03.117
I stole that joke which wasn't that great.

00:26:03.117 --> 00:26:04.430
(both chuckle)

00:26:04.430 --> 00:26:06.460
- I got this joke from
the people at the museum.

00:26:06.460 --> 00:26:08.910
- Nothing there in the
British museum is British.

00:26:09.930 --> 00:26:12.400
We were there for like an hour and then,

00:26:12.400 --> 00:26:14.830
we ended up going, we
went on the London Eye,

00:26:14.830 --> 00:26:16.590
the big Ferris wheel.

00:26:16.590 --> 00:26:18.110
Christy didn't do that.

00:26:18.110 --> 00:26:20.630
You're developing a
flavor profile of my wife.

00:26:20.630 --> 00:26:22.480
She doesn't like to fly but she did it.

00:26:22.480 --> 00:26:24.160
She conquered her fear,
it was a long flight.

00:26:24.160 --> 00:26:26.160
She conquers her fear every
single time she flies,

00:26:26.160 --> 00:26:28.820
so big ups to Christy.

00:26:28.820 --> 00:26:31.383
But she did not elect
to go on the London Eye.

00:26:32.618 --> 00:26:35.500
And I wasn't gonna,
you choose your battles

00:26:35.500 --> 00:26:36.480
and that wasn't it.

00:26:36.480 --> 00:26:38.170
Christy and I, Lily and I did that.

00:26:38.170 --> 00:26:39.820
- Well when you talk about these things,

00:26:39.820 --> 00:26:42.370
you need to tell me,
you need to give me the,

00:26:42.370 --> 00:26:44.180
I recommend that or I don't recommend that

00:26:44.180 --> 00:26:46.150
because I mean I'm gonna be in London

00:26:46.150 --> 00:26:48.150
in August and you know.

00:26:48.150 --> 00:26:50.500
- I recommend the British Museum.

00:26:50.500 --> 00:26:52.790
I wouldn't do the top 10 audio tour

00:26:52.790 --> 00:26:56.580
because they kinda send
you around to every part

00:26:56.580 --> 00:26:58.620
of the museum and that's what we did.

00:26:58.620 --> 00:27:02.550
It was very cool so we
kinda got a whirlwind tour

00:27:02.550 --> 00:27:05.268
but I would recommend
that you look at the types

00:27:05.268 --> 00:27:07.120
of things that you're interested in

00:27:07.120 --> 00:27:09.610
and just choose one or two and go there.

00:27:09.610 --> 00:27:11.673
Instead of trying to do the top
10 because there were things

00:27:11.673 --> 00:27:14.461
that I weren't quite as
interested in even though

00:27:14.461 --> 00:27:16.120
just for the sake of them wanting

00:27:16.120 --> 00:27:18.700
to take me around the whole museum.

00:27:18.700 --> 00:27:21.440
If you want a good place
to eat in Shoreditch,

00:27:21.440 --> 00:27:24.430
we ended up that first night
at a place called Gloria

00:27:25.840 --> 00:27:27.760
which had amazing pizza.

00:27:27.760 --> 00:27:30.603
Like really good pizza, really good vibe.

00:27:31.700 --> 00:27:33.000
I think there's one in Paris

00:27:33.000 --> 00:27:37.370
and then they just opened
this one in London and--

00:27:37.370 --> 00:27:39.080
- Do you meet Gloria?

00:27:39.080 --> 00:27:41.871
- No but they had records playing

00:27:41.871 --> 00:27:44.608
and the ceiling was mirrored.

00:27:44.608 --> 00:27:48.161
It was really cool, the
downstairs was awesome.

00:27:48.161 --> 00:27:51.126
- Is the London Eye worth it or is it--

00:27:51.126 --> 00:27:52.723
- I don't think so.
- It doesn't feel worth it

00:27:52.723 --> 00:27:54.090
to me--
- I went at night,

00:27:54.090 --> 00:27:55.140
it didn't feel worth it.

00:27:55.140 --> 00:27:57.320
I don't think it's worth it.
- I could imagine.

00:27:57.320 --> 00:27:58.510
- Yeah.
- I could imagine

00:27:58.510 --> 00:28:00.090
what it feels like, what it looks like.

00:28:00.090 --> 00:28:03.040
- It's very slow, it's not, ah, you know.

00:28:03.040 --> 00:28:05.884
- It's not the Zipper.
- It's missable.

00:28:05.884 --> 00:28:08.483
- It's not the Ring of Fire.

00:28:08.483 --> 00:28:11.959
- And then on day two, we
get up I'm like all right,

00:28:11.959 --> 00:28:13.917
we got our Oyster card from the Tube,

00:28:13.917 --> 00:28:16.075
I'm like we gotta get back on the Tube.

00:28:16.075 --> 00:28:18.640
I think what Christy
didn't tell me was that

00:28:18.640 --> 00:28:22.420
part of the fear of flying
is the confined space

00:28:22.420 --> 00:28:24.210
type situation so she wasn't really

00:28:24.210 --> 00:28:25.880
in love with the Tube either.

00:28:25.880 --> 00:28:29.547
I'm definitely not criticizing
her because I mean,

00:28:29.547 --> 00:28:33.190
there's some actual
anxiety going on in there,

00:28:33.190 --> 00:28:36.300
it's something that
like, I don't experience

00:28:36.300 --> 00:28:38.679
so it's hard for me to empathize

00:28:38.679 --> 00:28:41.010
but I'm definitely not
gonna fall for the trap

00:28:41.010 --> 00:28:42.650
of making fun of it.

00:28:42.650 --> 00:28:43.700
(chuckles)

00:28:43.700 --> 00:28:47.130
- Okay, then I will. (chuckles)

00:28:47.130 --> 00:28:50.970
- But so I'm like all right
let's get back on the Tube.

00:28:50.970 --> 00:28:53.194
We're gonna go to the Tower of London.

00:28:53.194 --> 00:28:55.950
We got the Harry Potter
stuff, just hold out,

00:28:55.950 --> 00:28:58.000
we'll get there tomorrow,
let's just get this,

00:28:58.000 --> 00:28:59.651
let's get this tourist
stuff out of the way

00:28:59.651 --> 00:29:01.570
that everybody does.

00:29:01.570 --> 00:29:04.185
And I'm kinda stressin'
out 'cause I'm like,

00:29:04.185 --> 00:29:05.900
it's taking me to different stops

00:29:05.900 --> 00:29:08.740
and Google maps are really
good at showing you which one

00:29:08.740 --> 00:29:11.157
to get on but like, then the train,

00:29:11.157 --> 00:29:13.240
it says it's about to pull
up and it says something

00:29:13.240 --> 00:29:14.523
a little bit different, and I'm like,

00:29:14.523 --> 00:29:16.480
I think this is it, let's
get on and then you get on

00:29:16.480 --> 00:29:19.000
and then you're like,
I'm starting to panic.

00:29:19.000 --> 00:29:20.487
And I'm like this is not the right one.

00:29:20.487 --> 00:29:23.220
And I'm like it's not
the right one and stop it

00:29:23.220 --> 00:29:24.870
at the first stop and I'm like--

00:29:24.870 --> 00:29:26.710
- Get off!
- We gotta get off.

00:29:26.710 --> 00:29:29.070
We gotta get off and we
were still like above ground

00:29:29.070 --> 00:29:30.760
at another platform.
- Uh-oh.

00:29:30.760 --> 00:29:33.740
- And I'm like this
is, I looked at the map

00:29:33.740 --> 00:29:34.860
where we were then, I was like, yeah,

00:29:34.860 --> 00:29:36.890
we went the wrong direction.

00:29:36.890 --> 00:29:39.190
And now it's saying we gotta
go and we can't just get

00:29:39.190 --> 00:29:41.180
on another train here

00:29:41.180 --> 00:29:44.470
and I'm like in full panic mode.

00:29:44.470 --> 00:29:47.317
Like--
(Rhett laughs)

00:29:47.317 --> 00:29:51.540
I actually was like falling
apart and I didn't realize it.

00:29:51.540 --> 00:29:56.240
I was panicking and
I'm like, we gotta get,

00:29:56.240 --> 00:29:58.870
'cause getting on a train and
then making a split decision

00:29:58.870 --> 00:30:01.370
to get off and make sure that all members

00:30:01.370 --> 00:30:02.410
of your family are there.

00:30:02.410 --> 00:30:04.090
Boy I'm glad I didn't
have the boys with me.

00:30:04.090 --> 00:30:05.974
I guarantee you at least
one of 'em would still be

00:30:05.974 --> 00:30:07.876
on that Tube right now.

00:30:07.876 --> 00:30:08.770
(chuckles)

00:30:08.770 --> 00:30:10.885
So we're on the platform--
- It'd be a good experience.

00:30:10.885 --> 00:30:14.630
- And Christy's like, I think,

00:30:14.630 --> 00:30:18.090
I'm like listen, we gotta,
you gotta get on this,

00:30:18.090 --> 00:30:20.240
we gotta go down here, we
gotta go on this platform,

00:30:20.240 --> 00:30:21.280
we gotta get on another train and we'll go

00:30:21.280 --> 00:30:22.590
in a totally different direction now

00:30:22.590 --> 00:30:26.460
because we're gonna be
late and she's like,

00:30:26.460 --> 00:30:30.540
why don't we just go up these
stairs and we could just Uber.

00:30:30.540 --> 00:30:31.898
And I'm like no!

00:30:31.898 --> 00:30:33.860
We can't Uber!

00:30:33.860 --> 00:30:35.380
I've got this card!

00:30:35.380 --> 00:30:38.650
I've invested in an Oyster card!

00:30:38.650 --> 00:30:42.760
I'm like, I'm not yelling at
Christy, I'm yelling at London.

00:30:42.760 --> 00:30:44.000
- Okay, yep.
- Okay?

00:30:44.000 --> 00:30:47.270
I'm yelling at myself I
think is what was happening.

00:30:47.270 --> 00:30:48.660
I was just freaking out and I was like,

00:30:48.660 --> 00:30:50.650
no we have to, we can do this.

00:30:50.650 --> 00:30:54.460
And like I can do this, I can, I can,

00:30:54.460 --> 00:30:56.320
I can navigate.

00:30:56.320 --> 00:30:58.670
I can get us there and it--

00:30:58.670 --> 00:30:59.980
- What was your daughter
doing at this point?

00:30:59.980 --> 00:31:02.150
- And she was like, she
was trying to be helpful.

00:31:02.150 --> 00:31:04.340
She was looking on her phone
and trying to figure out

00:31:04.340 --> 00:31:06.520
which train to get on because apparently,

00:31:06.520 --> 00:31:08.173
this is very important to me.

00:31:09.040 --> 00:31:13.518
And I said, "We can't take an Uber."

00:31:13.518 --> 00:31:15.750
I am just, and I'm looking at the phone

00:31:15.750 --> 00:31:18.640
and I'm trying to figure
it out and she's suggesting

00:31:19.670 --> 00:31:21.298
an alternate plan and I'm like,

00:31:21.298 --> 00:31:26.298
I'm gonna throw this
phone onto the tracks.

00:31:28.470 --> 00:31:31.620
And I like, at that point I locked eyes

00:31:31.620 --> 00:31:34.470
with Christy and I could tell that like,

00:31:34.470 --> 00:31:37.000
she could see that something was--

00:31:37.000 --> 00:31:40.230
- Snapping.
- Snapping inside of me

00:31:40.230 --> 00:31:45.220
and it was just like,
then she was like quiet.

00:31:45.220 --> 00:31:46.997
I looked at Lily and she looked in my eyes

00:31:46.997 --> 00:31:48.903
and she was like quiet and I was like,

00:31:51.610 --> 00:31:54.650
let's get an Uber.
(Rhett laughs)

00:31:54.650 --> 00:31:58.440
But it was interesting
how, and then we get

00:31:58.440 --> 00:32:01.053
to the Tower of London,
I'm thinking like, man,

00:32:02.120 --> 00:32:03.710
I'm trying to explain myself

00:32:03.710 --> 00:32:05.650
and like listen, it said it was gonna be

00:32:05.650 --> 00:32:10.251
a 20 minute subway ride or,
they don't use the word subway.

00:32:10.251 --> 00:32:12.050
- Oh that's your problem.
- 30 minute--

00:32:12.050 --> 00:32:13.896
- Calling it the wrong thing.
- Uber.

00:32:13.896 --> 00:32:16.690
And it was, we'd already
bought these and the price

00:32:16.690 --> 00:32:18.110
and all this stuff and
it's like all this stuff

00:32:18.110 --> 00:32:20.620
that doesn't matter, I was
just trying to make myself,

00:32:20.620 --> 00:32:23.780
I was trying to explain
myself and try to restore

00:32:23.780 --> 00:32:27.269
what I felt like was
the hope that was lost

00:32:27.269 --> 00:32:31.340
in my girl's eyes that I could lead us

00:32:31.340 --> 00:32:33.200
to the Tower of London,

00:32:33.200 --> 00:32:37.170
the place where queens
were beheaded or whatever.

00:32:37.170 --> 00:32:39.517
- Did you, I doubt that, did you--

00:32:40.551 --> 00:32:42.810
- Oh they beheaded grandmas.

00:32:42.810 --> 00:32:44.170
I took the tour, brother.

00:32:44.170 --> 00:32:45.820
They beheaded--
- Queens though?

00:32:46.730 --> 00:32:48.770
- A king and a king's mom.

00:32:48.770 --> 00:32:52.108
- But did you make it on
time, did you miss your tour?

00:32:52.108 --> 00:32:55.410
- Well a tour leaves every 30 minutes.

00:32:55.410 --> 00:32:57.123
- Okay.
- So again it's like--

00:32:57.123 --> 00:32:59.130
- It was okay.
- I was freaking out

00:32:59.130 --> 00:33:04.130
over nothing but it was like,
it was a bad Uber ride there,

00:33:05.100 --> 00:33:05.933
I will say that.

00:33:05.933 --> 00:33:07.668
It was like--
- This is day two.

00:33:07.668 --> 00:33:09.660
- Yeah, morning of day two.

00:33:09.660 --> 00:33:10.680
- Yeah can't have this kind of,

00:33:10.680 --> 00:33:13.030
you can't fall apart on day two.

00:33:13.030 --> 00:33:15.640
- We get out of the
Uber and Christy's like

00:33:15.640 --> 00:33:16.830
we're just gonna go get a coffee.

00:33:16.830 --> 00:33:18.280
I'm like well I'm gonna go get the tickets

00:33:18.280 --> 00:33:20.965
and then I'm like, I had some space and I,

00:33:20.965 --> 00:33:24.000
you know, I just needed
a couple of minutes

00:33:24.000 --> 00:33:25.920
to realize that I was being a jackass.

00:33:25.920 --> 00:33:28.130
- Well you know falling
