00:44:42.397 --> 00:44:43.907
"I gotta get these
adjusted when I get back

00:44:43.907 --> 00:44:45.250
"'cause it didn't work."

00:44:45.250 --> 00:44:46.083
- Okay.

00:44:46.083 --> 00:44:47.990
- But I put it back on
and said I'm gonna ski

00:44:47.990 --> 00:44:49.130
the rest of the afternoon.

00:44:49.130 --> 00:44:50.860
I get back out there, I also--
- You took it off,

00:44:50.860 --> 00:44:52.087
you didn't see anything wrong with it?

00:44:52.087 --> 00:44:54.240
I thought you were gonna say
he left a Dremel in there.

00:44:54.240 --> 00:44:55.430
- No.

00:44:55.430 --> 00:44:57.700
I take it off, nothing.

00:44:57.700 --> 00:45:01.270
I mean I take it off and my
foot instantly feels better.

00:45:01.270 --> 00:45:02.641
I put it back in there I'm like okay,

00:45:02.641 --> 00:45:05.190
this is good, I'll get it tight.

00:45:05.190 --> 00:45:07.940
Maybe it just needs to be tighter.

00:45:07.940 --> 00:45:09.350
'Cause he told me it's gotta be tight,

00:45:09.350 --> 00:45:11.356
you gotta get that thing tight in there.

00:45:11.356 --> 00:45:13.530
'Cause they got it perfect,
so I get it super tight.

00:45:13.530 --> 00:45:16.990
I ski a little bit more, I'm
having the time of my life.

00:45:16.990 --> 00:45:19.040
Let me just tell you, I
took a little pain reliever

00:45:19.040 --> 00:45:21.410
just to preemptively deal with the pain.

00:45:21.410 --> 00:45:23.010
- Yeah, yeah.

00:45:23.010 --> 00:45:25.900
- But we get down, we gotta
ski across the mountain

00:45:25.900 --> 00:45:27.981
to get back to where we were staying.

00:45:27.981 --> 00:45:31.386
The slopes close pretty early
there, around four o'clock.

00:45:31.386 --> 00:45:35.037
And so we were gonna come
about 3:30 and come into the,

00:45:35.037 --> 00:45:38.330
you literally, talk about being a douche,

00:45:38.330 --> 00:45:41.013
I mean you ski directly down the hill

00:45:41.013 --> 00:45:43.990
and you ski to the
valet guy for your hotel

00:45:43.990 --> 00:45:45.610
and you just give him
your ski and you walk in,

00:45:45.610 --> 00:45:48.610
he has hot chocolate, and
then you walk into your hotel.

00:45:48.610 --> 00:45:49.750
- Is that right?

00:45:49.750 --> 00:45:50.860
- It's all right there.

00:45:50.860 --> 00:45:51.693
It's unbelievable,

00:45:51.693 --> 00:45:54.060
it's the douchiest thing
you could possibly imagine.

00:45:54.060 --> 00:45:55.558
And there's douches everywhere.

00:45:55.558 --> 00:45:56.920
- Everywhere.
- Everywhere.

00:45:56.920 --> 00:45:59.030
Everywhere you look, a douche.

00:45:59.030 --> 00:46:00.356
- It's like lookin' in a mirror.

00:46:00.356 --> 00:46:01.589
(chuckles)

00:46:01.589 --> 00:46:03.533
- I mean people who ski in Vail,

00:46:03.533 --> 00:46:05.530
this is as douchey as it gets, man,

00:46:05.530 --> 00:46:07.280
as far as I've ever been around.

00:46:07.280 --> 00:46:08.946
- Yeah yeah, you were lovin' it.

00:46:08.946 --> 00:46:11.098
- And so--

00:46:11.098 --> 00:46:12.833
- I can do it.

00:46:12.833 --> 00:46:14.442
(Rhett laughs)

00:46:14.442 --> 00:46:17.570
- We're skiing back
to, it's a big mountain

00:46:17.570 --> 00:46:18.940
so we've got a lot of skiing to do

00:46:18.940 --> 00:46:21.253
and I get to a place where I'm like

00:46:21.253 --> 00:46:24.670
my foot is hurting me so
bad, but I can't show,

00:46:24.670 --> 00:46:27.120
I can't feel how much
pain I'm in to my boys.

00:46:27.120 --> 00:46:31.333
I gotta be the dad, I gotta hold this in.

00:46:31.333 --> 00:46:32.270
- Yeah.
- But there was a couple

00:46:32.270 --> 00:46:34.420
of times where we stopped,
where I would have to stop

00:46:34.420 --> 00:46:35.763
and wait oh Shepherd
'cause he was still kinda

00:46:35.763 --> 00:46:37.970
gettin' his legs and I would stop

00:46:37.970 --> 00:46:42.410
and the pain would just shoot
from my heel all the way

00:46:42.410 --> 00:46:44.110
up my leg and I would go (groans).

00:46:45.460 --> 00:46:47.110
It would be a moment of (groans),

00:46:47.110 --> 00:46:48.780
like crying out like
that and then I started--

00:46:48.780 --> 00:46:51.560
- Your eyes cross?
- Then I started thinking

00:46:51.560 --> 00:46:55.730
I may start crying I'm in so much pain.

00:46:55.730 --> 00:46:58.039
I hadn't cried from pain
since I was a child.

00:46:58.039 --> 00:46:59.918
(Link chuckles)

00:46:59.918 --> 00:47:01.650
- Really?
- But my eyes were watering,

00:47:01.650 --> 00:47:03.177
it was like oh gah!

00:47:04.030 --> 00:47:08.470
And then Locke was like,
Dad, do we need to get help?

00:47:08.470 --> 00:47:10.680
And I'm like, no son.

00:47:10.680 --> 00:47:13.560
Your dad's gonna make it to
the bottom of the mountain.

00:47:13.560 --> 00:47:15.555
- Your dad is a super douche.

00:47:15.555 --> 00:47:18.417
His super tight-fitted custom boots

00:47:18.417 --> 00:47:21.600
which don't amount to amount of crap.

00:47:21.600 --> 00:47:23.667
- Your dad will not be out-douched by any

00:47:23.667 --> 00:47:25.980
of the other douches around here.

00:47:25.980 --> 00:47:28.990
Your dad is the douchiest dad. (chuckles)

00:47:28.990 --> 00:47:31.990
- He's gonna cry but
he's gonna keep skiing

00:47:31.990 --> 00:47:33.930
all the way to his valet and get that,

00:47:33.930 --> 00:47:35.466
he's gonna grab that hot chocolate--

00:47:35.466 --> 00:47:37.201
- With tears in his eyes.
- With his tears in his eyes.

00:47:37.201 --> 00:47:39.540
(Rhett coughs)

00:47:39.540 --> 00:47:40.373
- So--

00:47:40.373 --> 00:47:42.990
- He's gonna retrieve
his testicles kept down

00:47:42.990 --> 00:47:44.080
at the base of the mountain.

00:47:44.080 --> 00:47:45.630
- I make it to the bottom of the mountain

00:47:45.630 --> 00:47:48.270
and I'm in intense pain,
I get my hot chocolate

00:47:48.270 --> 00:47:50.580
which makes me feel a little bit better.

00:47:50.580 --> 00:47:52.563
I walk back to my room.

00:47:53.971 --> 00:47:58.230
My wife is still out and about
doing whatever she wants.

00:47:58.230 --> 00:47:59.333
I take off my boot.

00:48:00.747 --> 00:48:04.003
And that, Link, is when--

00:48:04.003 --> 00:48:05.470
- The end of your story.

00:48:05.470 --> 00:48:09.230
- I did not know why I
didn't realize it before.

00:48:09.230 --> 00:48:11.680
But the little windshield
wiper that he had given me

00:48:11.680 --> 00:48:14.851
was in the bottom of my boot.
- No way!

00:48:14.851 --> 00:48:17.870
- And I have--
- Even when I said like--

00:48:17.870 --> 00:48:19.120
- No.
- I thought that you were

00:48:19.120 --> 00:48:20.820
looking for something at the restaurant.

00:48:20.820 --> 00:48:22.280
- This was in the bottom of my boot.

00:48:22.280 --> 00:48:24.293
- Oh my gosh.
- And let me show you

00:48:24.293 --> 00:48:26.077
what my foot looked like.

00:48:26.077 --> 00:48:28.258
- Oh!
(Rhett laughs)

00:48:28.258 --> 00:48:29.595
What?

00:48:29.595 --> 00:48:32.560
- It was, okay--
- You paid so much money

00:48:32.560 --> 00:48:34.862
for custom-fitted boots and then you put

00:48:34.862 --> 00:48:39.862
a plastic windshield wiper
directly under your heel.

00:48:42.530 --> 00:48:43.970
- Now you may think to yourself--

00:48:43.970 --> 00:48:46.126
- How stupid.
- How could you not feel

00:48:46.126 --> 00:48:47.610
that there was something under it?

00:48:47.610 --> 00:48:49.070
- Yeah.
- And something about

00:48:49.070 --> 00:48:52.170
the shape of this, the fact
that it was perfectly situated

00:48:52.170 --> 00:48:54.318
in the bottom of my heel,
the fact that it has

00:48:54.318 --> 00:48:56.750
absolutely no edge on one side

00:48:56.750 --> 00:49:00.120
and then it is a perfect
circle and the fact

00:49:00.120 --> 00:49:02.600
that I had experienced
almost the exact same type

00:49:02.600 --> 00:49:05.340
of pain before, all these factors led me

00:49:05.340 --> 00:49:07.420
to never consider that maybe the frickin'

00:49:07.420 --> 00:49:12.210
Skigee they call it, that's
funny, squeegee, Skigee.

00:49:12.210 --> 00:49:16.020
Is the thing that was sending
so much pain into my body.

00:49:16.020 --> 00:49:18.760
- And it's weird how it
was literally sending it

00:49:18.760 --> 00:49:20.580
directly up your heel.

00:49:20.580 --> 00:49:22.473
- It was crazy.

00:49:22.473 --> 00:49:26.653
- Like that's like an acupuncture
type situation, like--

00:49:26.653 --> 00:49:28.764
- And the fact that I
made the decision early on

00:49:28.764 --> 00:49:33.131
to be like, you know what,
it needs to be tighter.

00:49:33.131 --> 00:49:34.740
(chuckles) That was when the pain--

00:49:34.740 --> 00:49:36.770
- You ratcheted it down.
- Let me tell ya,

00:49:36.770 --> 00:49:39.100
the rest of the trip, when
I didn't have that thing

00:49:39.100 --> 00:49:41.330
in my boot, Claude.

00:49:41.330 --> 00:49:42.860
Shout-out to you, brother.

00:49:42.860 --> 00:49:45.000
You made my feet feel incredible.

00:49:45.000 --> 00:49:46.990
I mean you shouldn't
have given me that Skigee

00:49:46.990 --> 00:49:49.340
'cause I put it in my
boot, that was my fault,

00:49:49.340 --> 00:49:52.600
but the repairs you made
to my boots were on point.

00:49:52.600 --> 00:49:54.668
I don't need any adjustments.

00:49:54.668 --> 00:49:56.400
I'm so happy.

00:49:56.400 --> 00:49:58.780
It's the douchiest boots I've ever had.

00:49:58.780 --> 00:50:00.955
This is one happy douche.

00:50:00.955 --> 00:50:03.070
- And so that was day one.
- That was day one.

00:50:03.070 --> 00:50:04.460
I'm glad I got that out of the way.

00:50:04.460 --> 00:50:07.430
- Oh what if you'd realized
it at the end of the trip?

00:50:07.430 --> 00:50:08.620
That'd have been horrible.
- Well it would have been

00:50:08.620 --> 00:50:13.120
difficult to do because
I did look in my boot.

00:50:13.120 --> 00:50:14.990
I didn't look at it in the
restaurant 'cause I didn't want

00:50:14.990 --> 00:50:16.140
to even wanna draw attention to the fact

00:50:16.140 --> 00:50:17.950
that I was taking my boot off.

00:50:17.950 --> 00:50:20.400
- You were too concerned about your rep.

00:50:20.400 --> 00:50:22.210
You didn't want all the
other douches to know

00:50:22.210 --> 00:50:23.220
that you--
- Nobody else in

00:50:23.220 --> 00:50:25.300
the restaurant had their boots on at all.

00:50:25.300 --> 00:50:26.593
Nobody was even, well.

00:50:27.490 --> 00:50:29.242
- Oh really?
- Anyway.

00:50:29.242 --> 00:50:33.070
Skiing ended up being an
incredible part of the trip.

00:50:33.070 --> 00:50:35.511
I did yell at my children a little bit

00:50:35.511 --> 00:50:37.170
just to kinda join the club.

00:50:37.170 --> 00:50:38.820
- It's interesting that, I mean,

00:50:38.820 --> 00:50:41.230
you're the one who likes
variety and doesn't like

00:50:41.230 --> 00:50:42.890
to go back and do the same thing.

00:50:42.890 --> 00:50:46.120
I thought you're like man, this
skiing thing's getting old.

00:50:46.120 --> 00:50:47.450
I wanna experience something different.

00:50:47.450 --> 00:50:50.241
Are you doubling down on the skiing now?

00:50:50.241 --> 00:50:52.205
- No, at the end of the trip,

00:50:52.205 --> 00:50:56.340
my conclusion, and I
like to have a feeling

00:50:56.340 --> 00:50:59.840
and to sort of distill a
feeling at the end of the trip,

00:50:59.840 --> 00:51:01.810
and then kinda like remember that

00:51:01.810 --> 00:51:03.184
because you'll forget about it.

00:51:03.184 --> 00:51:05.320
It's just like when you have a child.

00:51:05.320 --> 00:51:07.210
After you have a child
you're like I'm never doing

00:51:07.210 --> 00:51:09.240
that again and then it wears
off and you have more kids.

00:51:09.240 --> 00:51:10.650
- Yeah you didn't distill it enough.

00:51:10.650 --> 00:51:12.093
- You gotta distill it.

00:51:12.990 --> 00:51:14.310
Distilled at the end of
the vacation, I was like,

00:51:14.310 --> 00:51:16.930
that was super fun but a couple things.

00:51:16.930 --> 00:51:20.520
I feel like I narrowly avoided
getting seriously hurt.

00:51:20.520 --> 00:51:22.170
Like just statistically speaking,

00:51:22.170 --> 00:51:24.420
there was so much skiing involved for me

00:51:24.420 --> 00:51:27.470
and my two sons that the fact
that none of us got hurt,

00:51:27.470 --> 00:51:28.940
I kinda feel like we got lucky

00:51:28.940 --> 00:51:31.011
'cause there was people getting
on our plane in wheelchairs

00:51:31.011 --> 00:51:33.270
and they had been recently injured.

00:51:33.270 --> 00:51:34.164
- Wow.

00:51:34.164 --> 00:51:36.210
- During their time in Vail.

00:51:36.210 --> 00:51:38.637
And also yeah, you do kinda feel like

00:51:38.637 --> 00:51:40.623
I've kinda been down this trail.

00:51:40.623 --> 00:51:42.730
And because I'm not some super risk-taker,

00:51:42.730 --> 00:51:44.623
I don't like to jump off of cliffs,

00:51:45.460 --> 00:51:47.880
my skiing experience sort of maxes out

00:51:47.880 --> 00:51:50.415
with a certain thing and so,

00:51:50.415 --> 00:51:53.690
and also Jessie didn't do it with us

00:51:53.690 --> 00:51:55.220
and so I kinda felt like I wasn't spending

00:51:55.220 --> 00:51:57.800
that much time with her so
I thought, you know what--

00:51:57.800 --> 00:52:00.120
- So you're saying this
phase is coming to an end?

00:52:00.120 --> 00:52:03.140
- No no no, no I think
that the weekend ski trip,

00:52:03.140 --> 00:52:05.169
the two day, the three day ski trip,

00:52:05.169 --> 00:52:08.623
but a full week of skiing in one place.

00:52:09.630 --> 00:52:10.818
We're not gonna do it next year,

00:52:10.818 --> 00:52:12.870
we're gonna do something
else, and then we'll decide

00:52:12.870 --> 00:52:14.830
as a family if we wanna
go back and do it again.

00:52:14.830 --> 00:52:16.801
Had an incredible time and the kids,

00:52:16.801 --> 00:52:18.960
one of the things about
doing that much skiing back

00:52:18.960 --> 00:52:22.510
to back is like, the learning curve.

00:52:22.510 --> 00:52:25.630
The kids are so much better.

00:52:25.630 --> 00:52:29.950
Shepherd can keep up with us perfectly now

00:52:29.950 --> 00:52:31.280
because he just did so much skiing.

00:52:31.280 --> 00:52:33.140
- Well you know I agree that you should

00:52:33.140 --> 00:52:34.530
distill down your trip.

00:52:34.530 --> 00:52:37.575
I think for me as it relates
to the trip, you know,

00:52:37.575 --> 00:52:41.230
I had all the expectations
of what the perfect trip

00:52:41.230 --> 00:52:44.540
would look like from an
itinerary standpoint.

00:52:44.540 --> 00:52:47.713
On the fifth day we went
to Stonehenge and Bath.

00:52:49.163 --> 00:52:51.597
And then just to give you a complete view

00:52:51.597 --> 00:52:53.930
of what my trip was, I do
recommend going to Stonehenge.

00:52:53.930 --> 00:52:55.950
I know you're into, it's not wood,

00:52:55.950 --> 00:52:57.180
but you're also into rocks--
- Yeah stones.

00:52:57.180 --> 00:53:01.650
- You're into like 5,000
year old rock configurations.

00:53:04.430 --> 00:53:05.350
Very cool.

00:53:05.350 --> 00:53:06.803
Bath was okay.

00:53:06.803 --> 00:53:09.303
(piano music)

00:53:17.920 --> 00:53:20.370
Tastes like bad pipes.

00:53:20.370 --> 00:53:23.010
Was cool to walk around but
I really loved Stonehenge.

00:53:23.010 --> 00:53:27.113
But as far as distilling it down,

00:53:28.050 --> 00:53:30.500
I also think I had a lot of expectations,

00:53:30.500 --> 00:53:33.540
I got really excited the
months back when I said

00:53:33.540 --> 00:53:37.736
this is gonna be like sweet 16, Lily trip.

00:53:37.736 --> 00:53:40.780
I don't know what I pictured specifically

00:53:40.780 --> 00:53:45.780
but I will be honest, and
this is not a criticism

00:53:46.004 --> 00:53:51.004
of Lily in any way, I
think it's more of just

00:53:51.650 --> 00:53:53.716
my internal dialogue that I don't know,

00:53:53.716 --> 00:53:56.450
I felt like there was
gonna be some magical,

00:53:56.450 --> 00:54:01.450
sweet 16 moment that I
found myself questioning.

00:54:01.700 --> 00:54:04.090
Should I have done
something more ceremonial?

00:54:04.090 --> 00:54:08.210
Like when we went to
Bath and Bath is the only

00:54:08.210 --> 00:54:11.090
naturally occurring
hot springs in the U.K.

00:54:11.090 --> 00:54:14.900
in that one town and they
built a whole, you know,

00:54:14.900 --> 00:54:15.733
town around it.

00:54:15.733 --> 00:54:20.420
People come forever and
just drink from the springs

00:54:20.420 --> 00:54:21.680
and they started bathing in it.

00:54:21.680 --> 00:54:23.310
I was like maybe I should do some sort

00:54:23.310 --> 00:54:25.740
of sweet 16 ceremony here--

00:54:25.740 --> 00:54:26.760
- I'm sure that would go over well.

00:54:26.760 --> 00:54:29.440
- Yeah it's like, that was
not my instinct at the time

00:54:29.440 --> 00:54:31.640
but then when it was
done, we were coming back,

00:54:31.640 --> 00:54:36.640
I was like, could it have
been even more special?

00:54:37.230 --> 00:54:40.850
I think I was thinking, I don't know,

00:54:40.850 --> 00:54:45.510
that there would all of
a sudden, the structure

00:54:48.263 --> 00:54:52.193
or the tone of our entire
relationship would shift.

00:54:55.650 --> 00:54:59.840
I ultimately chalked it up
to me having these nebulous

00:54:59.840 --> 00:55:04.840
but huge and unrooted
expectations of some magical event

00:55:06.150 --> 00:55:10.380
happening where oh look,
my daughter is a woman now

00:55:10.380 --> 00:55:13.910
and I guess that's not the
right way to say it. (chuckles)

00:55:13.910 --> 00:55:18.910
But like, that we'd have
this special moment,

00:55:18.910 --> 00:55:23.910
like remember that one
moment when she turned to me

00:55:24.600 --> 00:55:26.917
and her mom and said, "You know what,

00:55:26.917 --> 00:55:31.917
"you're the greatest parents
on Earth and I am so,

00:55:32.237 --> 00:55:36.227
"I just feel like I'm having
the most special moment

00:55:36.227 --> 00:55:38.867
"of my life right now and
I'm glad that I'm sharing it

00:55:38.867 --> 00:55:42.117
"with you and not with
some two-bit boyfriend

00:55:42.117 --> 00:55:46.450
"who wants to take me to some
knock-off prom situation."

00:55:46.450 --> 00:55:48.060
- Knock-off prom?
- I don't know I'm just--

00:55:48.060 --> 00:55:50.010
- What is that?
- There is no

00:55:50.010 --> 00:55:52.020
two-bit boyfriend and
there's no knock-off prom,

00:55:52.020 --> 00:55:56.450
I'm just saying like, you
know as your kids get older,

00:55:56.450 --> 00:56:00.076
at least for me, I want to
know that I have a vibrant

00:56:00.076 --> 00:56:03.430
and special relationship as it evolves,

00:56:03.430 --> 00:56:06.830
because make no mistake, it is evolving.

00:56:06.830 --> 00:56:08.730
She's becoming an adult.

00:56:08.730 --> 00:56:12.580
So it's not a parent-child
relationship exclusively,

00:56:12.580 --> 00:56:15.890
now it's like a, there's
a friend component,

00:56:15.890 --> 00:56:18.160
there's starting to have
conversations that you would have

00:56:18.160 --> 00:56:21.310
with any other adult and seeing her rise

00:56:21.310 --> 00:56:25.140
to that occasion
conversationally and say oh yeah,

00:56:25.140 --> 00:56:29.350
I'm seeing my daughter differently,
as a more mature person

00:56:29.350 --> 00:56:33.680
who's becoming her own
complete adult, you know?

00:56:33.680 --> 00:56:35.810
That's really starting
to happen at this point

00:56:35.810 --> 00:56:37.993
in an emotional, relational level.

00:56:40.500 --> 00:56:43.960
But I apparently had
some sort of expectation

00:56:43.960 --> 00:56:46.190
that there would be a magical moment.

00:56:46.190 --> 00:56:48.111
- That only happens in the movies, man.

00:56:48.111 --> 00:56:50.230
That happens on television.

00:56:50.230 --> 00:56:51.063
That doesn't happen in real life.

00:56:51.063 --> 00:56:55.340
- And to your point, if you
tried to ceremonialize it,

00:56:55.340 --> 00:56:56.970
it could go very sideways.

00:56:56.970 --> 00:56:59.590
It could be very awkward, you can't,

00:56:59.590 --> 00:57:04.590
so I kinda swung the pendulum
upon further reflection

00:57:05.270 --> 00:57:08.560
that if I would have
tried to create some sort

00:57:08.560 --> 00:57:13.560
of ceremonial moment like
manufacture it or engineer it,

00:57:14.093 --> 00:57:19.093
that the chances of that
backfiring into awkwardness

00:57:19.310 --> 00:57:24.310
and just seeming forced, there's
a high likelihood of that.

00:57:24.490 --> 00:57:29.070
So I think my takeaway in having
been awhile since the trip

00:57:30.440 --> 00:57:35.440
is just an appreciation
that we did the trip.

00:57:36.260 --> 00:57:39.310
I think as more time passes,
it will actually grow

00:57:39.310 --> 00:57:41.210
to be even more special.

00:57:41.210 --> 00:57:42.610
That's my theory at least,

00:57:42.610 --> 00:57:46.143
that you take the while time
as a whole and it's not,

00:57:47.440 --> 00:57:49.510
the picture that we took in
front of Westminster Abbey

00:57:49.510 --> 00:57:50.730
when it started hailing on us,

00:57:50.730 --> 00:57:52.130
maybe that'll be a great memory too.

00:57:52.130 --> 00:57:54.696
I think it will be, but
it's coming back after going

00:57:54.696 --> 00:57:59.410
to the Harry Potter tour all
day and riding in some huge

00:57:59.410 --> 00:58:03.264
frickin' torturous bus for over an hour

00:58:03.264 --> 00:58:05.590
to get to this place and to come back

00:58:05.590 --> 00:58:10.020
and then just crashing at our
room and saying you know what,

00:58:10.020 --> 00:58:11.890
let's just watch
something, and she's like,

00:58:11.890 --> 00:58:16.660
let's watch The Umbrella,
let's see, what's it called?

00:58:16.660 --> 00:58:18.000
Umbrella Academy.

00:58:18.000 --> 00:58:22.090
It's a series on Netflix that she's into.

00:58:22.090 --> 00:58:23.080
She's like I've already watched it

00:58:23.080 --> 00:58:24.880
but I wanna watch it with you guys.

00:58:24.880 --> 00:58:28.080
So it's like all three
of us piling into our bed

00:58:28.080 --> 00:58:31.560
and watching a Netflix series together.

00:58:31.560 --> 00:58:33.443
And I think those are the
things that I'm gonna remember,

00:58:33.443 --> 00:58:37.310
it's like, we hung out like friends

00:58:37.310 --> 00:58:42.310
and it was special and even,

00:58:42.640 --> 00:58:46.623
they may remember me having the breakdown.

00:58:48.460 --> 00:58:50.540
And I think we'll be able
to laugh about those type

00:58:50.540 --> 00:58:51.590
of things but I think it's--

00:58:51.590 --> 00:58:54.104
- It's an investment, man.
- It's an investment

00:58:54.104 --> 00:58:56.700
in setting an environment
where we can have

00:58:56.700 --> 00:58:59.120
an experience together so that

00:58:59.120 --> 00:59:01.860
the weird, the frustrating, the tired,

00:59:01.860 --> 00:59:05.660
and the special all kinda meld together

00:59:05.660 --> 00:59:09.685
and create something that
I believe is unforgettable

00:59:09.685 --> 00:59:14.685
but doesn't translate into
something you can just buy

00:59:15.030 --> 00:59:16.717
in a souvenir shop and
remember in that way.

00:59:16.717 --> 00:59:20.810
- It's not like something,
it's like investing

00:59:20.810 --> 00:59:23.040
into your retirement
fund and then going back

00:59:23.040 --> 00:59:26.300
the next day and looking at
it and being like oh, oh.

00:59:26.300 --> 00:59:28.660
When is this gonna grow?
- I think that's right.

00:59:28.660 --> 00:59:30.561
- Because I mean--
- And I also think

00:59:30.561 --> 00:59:34.940
from her perspective, it's
not like, I expected her

00:59:34.940 --> 00:59:36.503
to articulate things that--
- Kids don't do that.

00:59:36.503 --> 00:59:39.383
- As a 16 year old, she
has no point of reference.

00:59:40.564 --> 00:59:42.390
I didn't wanna go into
this lecture moment,

00:59:42.390 --> 00:59:44.900
like not every 16 year old
gets to go anywhere they want

00:59:44.900 --> 00:59:46.900
in the world and you get to go to London

00:59:46.900 --> 00:59:48.700
and don't forget what we've done for you.

00:59:48.700 --> 00:59:51.170
It's like I actually was
tempted to go into that

00:59:51.170 --> 00:59:52.800
a few times but--
- Well you should do that

00:59:52.800 --> 00:59:55.900
from time to time just to be a good dad.

00:59:55.900 --> 00:59:59.810
- Not that she wasn't
grateful or appreciative

00:59:59.810 --> 01:00:03.120
because she was but--
- You just have to hope

01:00:03.120 --> 01:00:05.360
that when the kids write
the tell-all book--

01:00:05.360 --> 01:00:06.193
- Uh-huh.

01:00:06.193 --> 01:00:08.722
- That the good outweighs the bad, man.

01:00:08.722 --> 01:00:11.590
And the best you can hope
for is that you're gonna get

01:00:11.590 --> 01:00:12.733
like a card.

01:00:13.820 --> 01:00:18.241
Okay, realistically,
you're gonna get a card

01:00:18.241 --> 01:00:21.500
when they're 33.
- Okay.

01:00:21.500 --> 01:00:24.630
- And they've got some life
experience and they're like,

01:00:24.630 --> 01:00:27.690
they write you a really
sweet card and they're like,

01:00:27.690 --> 01:00:32.060
I remember that trip and
I really thought about

01:00:33.080 --> 01:00:35.650
how you cared about us
and the time that you put

01:00:35.650 --> 01:00:39.337
into our relationship,
but unless you've got

01:00:39.337 --> 01:00:44.337
an exceptionally exceptional,
unusual, expressive child,

01:00:47.490 --> 01:00:49.780
they're not gonna be
like, I wanna let you know

01:00:49.780 --> 01:00:52.807
that this vacation is
going incredibly well

01:00:52.807 --> 01:00:55.330
and I recognize--
- Right.

01:00:55.330 --> 01:00:57.350
- That you guys have
actually thought about this

01:00:57.350 --> 01:01:02.310
in a way that, I realize
this is just for me.

01:01:02.310 --> 01:01:05.640
You guys made a decision to
leave the other kids at home,

01:01:05.640 --> 01:01:08.630
to just bring me here to do
the things that I wanted to do,

01:01:08.630 --> 01:01:10.713
to create a memory for me.

01:01:11.830 --> 01:01:13.340
You guys are the best
parents in the world.

01:01:13.340 --> 01:01:15.290
- Yeah that's what I wanted
and that would be weird,

01:01:15.290 --> 01:01:16.130
wouldn't it?

01:01:16.130 --> 01:01:19.230
- You don't want that
because then it's about you.

01:01:19.230 --> 01:01:20.450
- Mm.

01:01:20.450 --> 01:01:21.660
Isn't that true.

01:01:21.660 --> 01:01:24.020
- It's not about you,
it's about the investment

01:01:24.020 --> 01:01:26.103
that you're making in your
daughter and your family--

01:01:26.103 --> 01:01:27.410
- That's right.
- And then you gotta hope

01:01:27.410 --> 01:01:29.210
that not only is it, first
of all, you don't do it

01:01:29.210 --> 01:01:31.990
just for the card or for the tell-all book

01:01:31.990 --> 01:01:34.630
to be good versus bad, you're doing it

01:01:34.630 --> 01:01:35.950
because you actually
think it's contributing

01:01:35.950 --> 01:01:36.960
to her as a person.

01:01:36.960 --> 01:01:38.290
- And how can she know?

01:01:38.290 --> 01:01:39.970
How could she even process?

01:01:39.970 --> 01:01:44.631
At my age, I can barely
process the way that

01:01:44.631 --> 01:01:47.160
my experiences are impacting who I am.

01:01:47.160 --> 01:01:48.423
- Yeah you take me for
granted all the time.

01:01:48.423 --> 01:01:50.299
- Yeah. (chuckles)

01:01:50.299 --> 01:01:52.949
- No I mean I deal with the
same things with my kids.

01:01:53.860 --> 01:01:57.993
No, I do on a regular
basis and also think that,

01:01:58.970 --> 01:02:00.593
generalization here but,

01:02:02.374 --> 01:02:06.213
dealing with two boys is
a little bit different.

01:02:08.683 --> 01:02:12.140
(sighs) They are constantly fighting

01:02:12.140 --> 01:02:14.960
and constantly annoying each other

01:02:14.960 --> 01:02:19.440
and we had to, there were multiple times

01:02:19.440 --> 01:02:20.450
where I'm just like--

01:02:20.450 --> 01:02:21.570
(grunting angrily)

01:02:21.570 --> 01:02:22.920
Going to that kinda Dad mode, you know.

01:02:22.920 --> 01:02:25.540
And then I have to pull myself back.

01:02:25.540 --> 01:02:27.830
But you talk about you
guys getting together.

01:02:27.830 --> 01:02:30.500
One of the most special
things that we experienced

01:02:30.500 --> 01:02:33.660
on our trip was, okay so every day,

01:02:33.660 --> 01:02:35.270
three of us would go out and ski.

01:02:35.270 --> 01:02:37.700
There would be multiple
fights between Locke

01:02:37.700 --> 01:02:39.810
and Shepherd because Locke's getting mad

01:02:39.810 --> 01:02:43.310
that Shepherd's holding
us up and he falls down

01:02:43.310 --> 01:02:45.060
and he needs help and other
people have to help him

01:02:45.060 --> 01:02:47.116
if I'm below him on
the mountain and stuff,

01:02:47.116 --> 01:02:49.357
and then Locke'll be like,
I'm gonna ski by myself.

01:02:49.357 --> 01:02:51.700
And so he goes off and
does his teenage ski

01:02:51.700 --> 01:02:53.360
by himself thinking
that Shepherd and I ski

01:02:53.360 --> 01:02:54.510
and then we'll meet up.

01:03:09.499 --> 01:03:11.249
You 'bout to do that?

01:03:13.220 --> 01:03:14.470
You gonna come down that?

01:03:15.440 --> 01:03:18.210
- [Shepherd] Which way are we going?

01:03:18.210 --> 01:03:19.510
- Oh, nothing.

01:03:19.510 --> 01:03:21.250
So that kind of thing is happening.

01:03:21.250 --> 01:03:22.950
I'm kinda trying to manage
it and I'm not trying

01:03:22.950 --> 01:03:24.930
to force it because I want the same thing.

01:03:24.930 --> 01:03:27.186
I want there to be these moments of like,

01:03:27.186 --> 01:03:30.010
a father and his two
sons in perfect rhythm

01:03:30.010 --> 01:03:31.620
on the slopes of Colorado.

01:03:31.620 --> 01:03:32.630
- Yeah.
- Postcard.

01:03:32.630 --> 01:03:34.073
- Yeah.
- But really it's--

01:03:34.073 --> 01:03:35.710
- And while they're skiing
they're looking at you

01:03:35.710 --> 01:03:37.300
talking about how great of a dad you are.

01:03:37.300 --> 01:03:39.850
- Yeah, right, but really
what's happening is

01:03:39.850 --> 01:03:41.830
I'm getting separated from Shepherd

01:03:41.830 --> 01:03:43.590
and I'm getting a phone call from Jessie

01:03:43.590 --> 01:03:45.970
who got a phone call from the
guy at the lift who's like,

01:03:45.970 --> 01:03:48.040
your son is here and is lost.

01:03:48.040 --> 01:03:50.320
That happened, yeah, it's
not even worth (chuckles).

01:03:50.320 --> 01:03:52.310
- Shepherd or Locke?
- Shepherd.

01:03:52.310 --> 01:03:55.330
Locke had his own phone,
but Shepherd was fine.

01:03:55.330 --> 01:03:56.970
He didn't panic or anything,
he knew that we would get

01:03:56.970 --> 01:03:59.540
in touch when we got back up.

01:03:59.540 --> 01:04:02.227
And also in the midst
of looking for Shepherd,

01:04:02.227 --> 01:04:05.540
cutting off this little girl
and her dad come skiing up

01:04:05.540 --> 01:04:08.907
to me and he's like what
the hell were you thinking?

01:04:08.907 --> 01:04:10.840
And me not saying anything back to him

01:04:10.840 --> 01:04:12.650
because I didn't have an answer.

01:04:12.650 --> 01:04:15.220
- There's something in my boot.

01:04:15.220 --> 01:04:16.910
- No that was after the boot had been--

01:04:16.910 --> 01:04:19.350
- There was something
in my boot yesterday.

01:04:19.350 --> 01:04:21.270
- But the cool, one of the
best things that happened

01:04:21.270 --> 01:04:22.920
as a family is we would go skiing

01:04:22.920 --> 01:04:25.280
and then we would come back
and we would go out to eat.

01:04:25.280 --> 01:04:27.620
And then we would come back to the hotel

01:04:27.620 --> 01:04:29.820
and they had their room
and we had our room.

01:04:30.830 --> 01:04:34.700
But we would all get into
our bed so four of us,

01:04:34.700 --> 01:04:38.090
which is even more difficult
than three people in a bed

01:04:38.960 --> 01:04:40.910
and we would watch The Goldbergs.

01:04:40.910 --> 01:04:42.100
Now I know--
- The Goldbergs.

01:04:42.100 --> 01:04:45.517
- I've already plugged The Goldbergs once.

01:04:45.517 --> 01:04:47.920
But--
- You pluggin' some Goldbergs.

01:04:47.920 --> 01:04:50.040
That was your show.
- And again,

01:04:50.040 --> 01:04:52.980
let me just say that I'm not saying

01:04:52.980 --> 01:04:55.160
that it's a perfect show, I'm
saying it is the perfect show

01:04:55.160 --> 01:05:00.160
to watch as our family
because we all fit these

01:05:01.094 --> 01:05:04.730
totally exaggerated tropes and
the show is so exaggerated,

01:05:04.730 --> 01:05:05.660
well not really, I mean it's--

01:05:05.660 --> 01:05:06.940
- You don't have to go back through why

01:05:06.940 --> 01:05:09.350
The Goldbergs is great but
what did it do for you guys?

01:05:09.350 --> 01:05:14.350
- No but we relate, there's
something about all this stuff

01:05:15.210 --> 01:05:17.650
that we've done during
the day of Locke being

01:05:17.650 --> 01:05:19.440
the typical teenager and Shepherd being

01:05:19.440 --> 01:05:20.862
the typical annoying little brother

01:05:20.862 --> 01:05:23.740
and Jessie being the overprotective mom

01:05:23.740 --> 01:05:26.930
and me being the fad with a short fuse--

01:05:26.930 --> 01:05:28.281
- Uh-huh.
- That when you sit down

01:05:28.281 --> 01:05:32.530
and you see it played out
comedically in a show,

01:05:32.530 --> 01:05:36.680
we start realizing how we've
just fallen into these roles

01:05:36.680 --> 01:05:39.930
in these predictable ways
and it just makes us,

01:05:39.930 --> 01:05:43.500
we bonded as a family
and then like Shepherd,

01:05:43.500 --> 01:05:45.230
it'd be like 10 o'clock at night

01:05:45.230 --> 01:05:47.880
and Shepherd figured out
how to call room to room

01:05:47.880 --> 01:05:49.320
of course so we started doing that a lot.

01:05:49.320 --> 01:05:51.230
The phone would ring, I'd
pick it up and be like hello

01:05:51.230 --> 01:05:53.010
and Shepherd would be like,
you know what time it is.

01:05:53.010 --> 01:05:54.646
I was like what, he said, "Goldberg time."

01:05:54.646 --> 01:05:59.610
(laughs) So he was like,
he was so into us--

01:05:59.610 --> 01:06:00.730
- That's good.
- Sitting down

01:06:00.730 --> 01:06:01.690
and there's not a--
- It's kinda like

01:06:01.690 --> 01:06:03.363
our Survivor time.

01:06:03.363 --> 01:06:04.590
- Like you've said before,

01:06:04.590 --> 01:06:08.530
there's not a lot of
entertainment out there

01:06:08.530 --> 01:06:09.840
that you can just sit down as a family

01:06:09.840 --> 01:06:11.340
and just completely bond.

01:06:11.340 --> 01:06:12.790
That used to be the way it was.

01:06:12.790 --> 01:06:14.350
- Yeah.
- Ironically back in the 80s

01:06:14.350 --> 01:06:17.120
which is when The Goldbergs is based but

01:06:17.120 --> 01:06:18.920
that was the sweetest time that we had.

01:06:18.920 --> 01:06:21.620
Lots of typical family stuff that we did

01:06:21.620 --> 01:06:22.837
of getting on each other's nerves

01:06:22.837 --> 01:06:25.180
but there was that time
we would all get together,

01:06:25.180 --> 01:06:27.870
all file into the bed and just,

01:06:27.870 --> 01:06:28.953
I think that that's the kinda thing

01:06:28.953 --> 01:06:30.720
that they're gonna remember.
- Yeah.

01:06:30.720 --> 01:06:32.510
- They're gonna have a memory of like,

01:06:32.510 --> 01:06:36.140
I remember being, you don't
remember being annoyed

01:06:36.140 --> 01:06:38.270
by your little brother or your big brother

01:06:38.270 --> 01:06:41.765
or your dad having a short
fuse or your mom picking

01:06:41.765 --> 01:06:44.580
at something on you and upsetting you.

01:06:44.580 --> 01:06:49.470
I think you remember
those, the sweet times.

01:06:49.470 --> 01:06:53.230
- And if they don't, we can
make 'em listen to this podcast.

01:06:53.230 --> 01:06:54.400
- You have anything else from London?

01:06:54.400 --> 01:06:56.330
- No that's it, man.

01:06:56.330 --> 01:06:58.890
We rode the double-decker bus.

01:06:58.890 --> 01:07:00.760
Christy liked that a lot
better than the Tube.

01:07:00.760 --> 01:07:02.914
A lot more visibility.

01:07:02.914 --> 01:07:04.690
Everybody was happy--
- On top?

01:07:04.690 --> 01:07:07.720
- Once we get up top of
the double-decker bus.

01:07:07.720 --> 01:07:09.551
- I have a quick rec that I think

01:07:09.551 --> 01:07:10.950
you're gonna whole-heartedly agree with.

01:07:10.950 --> 01:07:13.293
♪ Check baby check baby
one two three four ♪

01:07:13.293 --> 01:07:14.143
♪ Check baby check baby ♪

01:07:14.143 --> 01:07:14.976
- I do--

01:07:14.976 --> 01:07:15.809
♪ One two ♪

01:07:15.809 --> 01:07:17.260
- Just as a small addendum because

01:07:17.260 --> 01:07:19.363
I can't not tell you this.

01:07:20.460 --> 01:07:23.091
In the little shop in Vail--
- There's a rec?

01:07:23.091 --> 01:07:25.010
- This is a--
- You teased the rec.

01:07:25.010 --> 01:07:25.843
- I'm teasing the rec.

01:07:25.843 --> 01:07:27.770
- Okay.
- In the little shop in Vail,

01:07:27.770 --> 01:07:31.210
Vail Village, well in Vail
Village there's a bunch of shops

01:07:31.210 --> 01:07:36.210
and there's a bunch of
really rich folk stuff.

01:07:36.361 --> 01:07:37.970
- They call those boutiques.

01:07:37.970 --> 01:07:42.150
- And we went into this
one store called Gorsuch,

01:07:42.150 --> 01:07:43.470
which I just thought that he was the guy

01:07:43.470 --> 01:07:44.770
that Obama tried to get to be

01:07:44.770 --> 01:07:46.480
the Supreme Court nominee.
- Neil Gorsuch.

01:07:46.480 --> 01:07:48.433
- It didn't work out too well.

01:07:49.295 --> 01:07:52.793
But apparently it's a
brand or it's a store.

01:07:53.921 --> 01:07:57.530
And you could kinda tell from the window

01:07:57.530 --> 01:08:00.900
that this was like douche central.

01:08:00.900 --> 01:08:01.900
And so we're like let's go--

01:08:01.900 --> 01:08:03.600
- You were like a moth to a flame.

01:08:03.600 --> 01:08:04.710
- Well here's the thing.

01:08:04.710 --> 01:08:06.730
I'll get custom ski
boots but I don't go in

01:08:06.730 --> 01:08:09.000
and buy unnecessarily expensive clothing.

01:08:09.000 --> 01:08:11.000
I don't do that.
- Like a vest

01:08:11.000 --> 01:08:13.248
with like skiers on it.
- I don't do that.

01:08:13.248 --> 01:08:15.210
So we go into this place.

01:08:15.210 --> 01:08:16.620
- You went in.

01:08:16.620 --> 01:08:19.120
- For entertainment purposes,
I'm not gonna buy anything.

01:08:19.120 --> 01:08:20.988
I'm going in there because I'm blown away

01:08:20.988 --> 01:08:25.490
at the culture and like the
vibe and it's three stories.

01:08:25.490 --> 01:08:28.010
- I think the collars won't un-pop there.

01:08:28.010 --> 01:08:30.710
- We go in there, you walk in,

01:08:30.710 --> 01:08:32.860
the first level is the women's level.

01:08:32.860 --> 01:08:34.450
